/* ===========================================================================
 * Crimson Slate -- the "Ironforge" palette for Noggit Crimson
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Part of Noggit Crimson, licensed under the GNU General Public License v3.
 *
 * The DIRECTORY is still CrimsonSlate and must stay that way: QSettings stores
 * the chosen theme by folder name, so renaming it silently resets every
 * existing user to the default theme. Crimson survives in the palette, but as
 * the DESTRUCTIVE rank only -- the accent is gold.
 *
 * Drop-in theme: the settings panel lists any directory under themes/ that
 * contains a theme.qss. Every url() below is written as
 *   themes/CrimsonSlate/images/<file>.png
 * and must stay that way -- the loader rewrites that exact prefix so the paths
 * resolve against the executable directory rather than the working directory.
 *
 * The 34 PNGs in images/ are generated, not drawn. Edit the colour constants at
 * the top of generate_icons.py (the same tokens as below), then run
 *   python generate_icons.py images
 * from this directory. Stdlib only -- no Pillow, no ImageMagick, no network.
 *
 * check_qss.py in this directory is a static checker for this file. Qt silently
 * ignores anything it does not understand, so run it after every edit:
 *   python check_qss.py theme.qss .
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * PALETTE. Change these values and nothing else to retheme.
 * ===========================================================================
 * The same values build the application QPalette in ApplicationEntry.cpp. If
 * you change one here, change it there too, or an unstyled widget will not
 * match a styled one.
 *
 * HUE DIRECTION. Every neutral is built at hue 30-40 degrees and 6-19%
 * saturation -- warm slate, not photocopier grey. Blizzard chrome is warm iron
 * and old leather lit by gold. Gold is also the only accent that stays fully
 * saturated at the lightness this palette needs; a blue at the same luminance
 * washes out (#6FAEDC is only 61% saturated at L=0.388), so the choice is
 * arithmetic, not taste.
 *
 * SURFACES -- the depth model. The window RECEDES, the panel is the working
 * plane, things you PRESS come forward, fields you TYPE INTO go back to the
 * floor, and things that FLOAT come furthest forward.
 *   bg.void       #100E0B   3D viewport surround, dock gutters, splitters,
 *                           menu bar, status bar, the title bar band -- AND
 *                           the inside of every input well, item view and
 *                           progress track. Also the ink drawn on an accent
 *                           fill. NO LONGER the scroll trough: that is
 *                           transparent now, see QScrollBar.
 *   bg.alt        #1D1916   alternating item-view row; focused input fill
 *   bg.panel      #292621   dock bodies, dialog bodies, tab panes, sections
 *   bg.raised     #3C3732   buttons, header sections, spin/combo sub-buttons,
 *                           docked toolbars
 *   bg.overlay    #4A4640   menus, combo popups, tool tips, the floating cards
 *                           drawn over the 3D view
 *   bg.hover      #5B5651   hover plate on an overlay surface. ICONS AND SHORT
 *                           LABELS ONLY -- body text on it is 5.47:1 and this
 *                           sheet's floor for prose is 7:1.
 *
 * ADJACENT STEP           MEASURED     PREVIOUS SHEET
 *   void    -> panel      1.279:1      1.064
 *   panel   -> raised     1.281:1      1.101
 *   raised  -> overlay    1.256:1      1.128
 *   overlay -> hover      1.292:1      n/a
 *   void    -> alt        1.104:1      deliberately subtle; zebra only
 *   TOTAL void -> overlay 2.057:1      1.321
 * The old sheet's four "levels" spanned 1.321:1 in total, which is one surface
 * wearing four names -- that is why the application read as a wireframe of
 * itself. This ladder is 2.5x longer in ratio terms.
 *
 * WHY THE TOP SURFACE IS NOT LIGHTER: the 7:1 body-text floor binds.
 * Body text #E4DFD7 on bg.overlay #4A4640 is 7.065:1; on #4B4741 it is 6.955 and
 * fails. Every surface above bg.overlay breaks menu legibility, which is why
 * bg.hover carries icons and short labels and never prose.
 *
 * STROKES. THE EDGE RAMP WAS REBUILT AND THAT IS THE ONLY PALETTE CHANGE IN
 * THIS REVISION. The old ramp topped out at stroke #565049, which measures
 * 1.479:1 against the bg.raised fill of the button it outlines and 1.894:1
 * against the bg.panel behind it. Neither clears the 3:1 floor WCAG 2.1 SC
 * 1.4.11 sets for a graphical object, so the control edge -- the single most
 * repeated mark in the application, on every button, field, combo, spin box
 * and colour swatch -- was not there at all. Three cues identified a button
 * and all three measured under the floor: border vs its own fill 1.479:1,
 * border vs the surround 1.894:1, fill vs the surround 1.281:1. That is why
 * successive passes of colour work read as invisible; the colours moved and
 * the EDGES never appeared.
 *   stroke.soft   #403B35   hairline SEAMS inside a panel or a void well, and
 *                           the disabled edge. 1.36:1 on panel, 1.74:1 on
 *                           void. A seam is not an object and does not carry
 *                           3:1. NOT permitted on bg.raised (1.06:1) or
 *                           bg.overlay (1.18:1).
 *   stroke        #565049   the grounded BOTTOM of a bevel, disabled fills,
 *                           and the texture-ramp groove outline whose radius
 *                           and colour are pinned to DesignTokens.hpp. 1.89:1
 *                           on panel, 1.48:1 on raised, 2.42:1 on void. It is
 *                           no longer the edge of an enabled control.
 *   stroke.hi     #746D64   every separator drawn ON bg.overlay, where it is
 *                           1.84:1 and stroke is 1.18:1, and the edge of a
 *                           floating card. Not an enabled-control edge either:
 *                           2.31:1 on raised, 2.95:1 on panel.
 *   edge          #8A8378   THE VISIBLE EDGE OF AN ENABLED CONTROL. The
 *                           lightest neutral that still reads as a line rather
 *                           than as text, measured on every surface a control
 *                           can sit on: void 5.138:1, alt 4.654:1, panel
 *                           4.018:1, raised 3.137:1 -- all four clear 3:1,
 *                           where the token it replaces cleared none of them.
 *                           BARRED FROM bg.overlay (2.498:1) and bg.hover
 *                           (1.934:1): menus, tool tips and the floating cards
 *                           keep stroke.hi.
 *   edge.lit      #A9A296   the LIT top edge of the one-pixel bevel. 4.648:1
 *                           on the bg.raised button fill, 5.953:1 on panel,
 *                           3.701:1 on bg.overlay, and 3.143:1 against the
 *                           stroke that grounds the bottom of the same button
 *                           -- so the bevel is a legible step and not a
 *                           1.05:1 rumour. On the pressed bg.void fill the
 *                           bevel inverts and reads 7.613:1.
 * THE HOVER EDGE is text.dim #BFB7AA used as a stroke, the same way ink is
 * bg.void used as a pen. 5.922:1 on bg.raised, 4.716:1 on bg.overlay, 9.699:1
 * on bg.void, and 1.888:1 above edge, so rest -> hover is a felt step in the
 * same direction the fill moves. stroke.hi could not serve: at 2.305:1 on
 * bg.raised it is DARKER than edge and a hover would have read as a recess.
 * DISABLED came free: stroke.soft is now 2.954:1 below edge, against 1.393:1
 * below the old stroke, so a disabled control is unmistakable without a second
 * cue.
 *
 * TEXT, four ranks. Measured void / panel / raised / overlay:
 *   text.hi       #F3F0E9   16.93  13.24  10.34   8.23   headings, dock
 *                           titles, values being edited, hovered labels
 *   text          #E4DFD7   14.53  11.36   8.87   7.07   body -- the default
 *   text.dim      #BFB7AA    9.70   7.58   5.92   4.72   captions, units,
 *                           header sections, the unchecked icon pen
 *   text.off      #7F786A    4.40   3.44   2.69   2.14   disabled only
 * Body clears 7:1 on every surface it is permitted on. text.dim clears 4.5:1
 * on all four and is barred from bg.hover (3.65:1). text.off is the one
 * documented exception: WCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.3 exempts inactive components and
 * sub-threshold IS the disabled signal. It is never used for enabled text.
 *
 * ACCENT -- ONE meaning: "the thing you are acting on". Selection, focus,
 * checked, the active tool, slider fill, progress, the tab underline, the dock
 * title rule. The accent moved from crimson to gold because crimson cannot be
 * both "selected" and "will destroy your data": the old accent #E8543F and the
 * old error #E0574B measured 1.024:1 apart in luminance and 4.0 degrees in
 * hue, i.e. the same colour, which is why red meant eight different things.
 * Gold against crimson is 34.5 degrees and 1.389:1.
 *   accent        #DFA52E   ink on it 8.77:1 (crimson managed only 4.88:1)
 *   accent.hi     #F0BA4A   hover on a FILLED accent surface; ink 10.85:1
 *   accent.press  #B8801F   pressed on a FILLED accent surface; ink 5.64:1
 *   ink           #100E0B   = bg.void. The text drawn ON any accent or status
 *                           fill. Note the press no longer has to invert to
 *                           light text the way crimson did.
 * Accent as a 2px/3px RULE on bg.overlay is 4.27:1, over the 3:1 floor for a
 * non-text state indicator. Washes: 0.24 alpha for "selected", 0.34 for
 * "selected AND hovered", permitted on bg.panel and darker only.
 *
 * STATUS -- meaning, never decoration.
 *   ok            #4FB87E   panel 6.09  raised 4.76  ink-on-it 7.79
 *   warn          #E2803C   panel 5.30  raised 4.14  ink-on-it 6.78
 *   bad           #E86F62   panel 4.94  raised 3.86  ink-on-it 6.31
 *   info          #6FAEDC   panel 6.29  raised 4.91  ink-on-it 8.04
 *   bad.press     #C85F54   the destructive button pressed; ink on it 4.79.
 *                           It was #C4574A, which put ink at 4.41 -- under the
 *                           4.5 floor on an ENABLED control, so the disabled
 *                           exemption did not apply. Lightened until ink
 *                           clears, and it is still 1.32:1 below bad, so the
 *                           press step is as legible as it was.
 *   close.hover   #A75047   title-bar close, hovered. See below: that one
 *                           glyph is a polish-time PNG and cannot restate
 *                           itself per pseudo-state, so it stays text.hi and
 *                           the FILL is what moved. text.hi on it 4.75:1, and
 *                           the 40px plate reads 3.56:1 against the bg.void
 *                           bar it sits in.
 *   close.press   #974840   title-bar close, pressed. text.hi 5.56:1, plate
 *                           vs bar 3.04:1, and 1.17:1 below close.hover.
 *   bad.tint      #2E1C17   bad at 14% alpha over bg.void -- an error field
 *   warn.tint     #2D1E12   warn at 14% alpha over bg.void -- a warning field
 * Status colours are TEXT only on bg.void and bg.panel, where all four clear
 * 4.5:1. On bg.raised and bg.overlay they appear as 1px borders and as fills,
 * where the floor is 3:1 and all four clear it.
 * SEPARATION: accent vs bad 34.5 deg / 1.389:1, vs ok 106.5 deg, vs info
 * 165.0 deg. HONEST LIMIT: accent vs warn is 15.8 deg and warn vs bad is 18.8.
 * Four warm signals cannot all be 30 degrees apart. warn and bad are an
 * escalation pair and adjacency is the traffic-light convention. accent vs
 * warn is disambiguated by FORM and this is a hard rule: a warn or error input
 * takes a coloured border AND a 14%-alpha tinted fill; a focused input takes a
 * 1px accent border and NEVER a tint. Never rely on the hue alone.
 *
 * The texture-level slider value ramp, the one gradient in this file:
 *   ramp.hi       #F2F0EC   top of the ramp
 *   ramp.lo       #0C0A08   bottom of the ramp
 * Neither is pure white or pure black -- the editor has to stay legible beside
 * a sunlit viewport, and pure endpoints bloom against it.
 *
 * THE BRAND RANK -- the project-selection window and brand furniture ONLY.
 * This is a product identity, not a second accent, and the rule that keeps it
 * honest is set out in full at the launcher section near the foot of this file.
 * It appears on exactly one window, on the banner, the wordmark, the primary
 * action's fill and border, the section ticks and a selected project card.
 * Nothing in the editor's chrome, none of the 34 generated icons, no
 * DesignTokens value and no QPalette role moves.
 *   brand.crimson    #E5405C   hue 349.8, sat 76.0%, L 57.5%. void 4.790,
 *                              band.left 4.620, band.right 4.939, panel 3.746.
 *                              BARRED FROM bg.raised: 2.925, under the 3:1
 *                              graphical floor. Ink on it 4.790; text.hi on it
 *                              3.535, so a SOLID crimson surface carries ink
 *                              and never light text.
 *   brand.crimson.hi #F05A73   hover on a brand border. 5.887 on void, 4.689
 *                              on the hover fill below, and 1.229:1 above
 *                              brand.crimson so the step is felt.
 *   band.left        #17120F   the banner band at its lit end
 *   band.right       #0B0908   and at its dark end. 1.069:1 apart -- a felt
 *                              darkening, not a boundary.
 *   brand.fill       #2E1516   brand.crimson at 14% straight sRGB alpha over
 *                              bg.void. text.hi on it 14.940, the crimson
 *                              border on it 4.226.
 *   brand.fill.hi    #3F191D   at 22%. text.hi 13.487, crimson.hi border 4.689,
 *                              and 1.108:1 above brand.fill.
 *   brand.fill.lo    #501D23   at 30%. text.hi 11.973, crimson border 3.387,
 *                              and 1.248:1 above brand.fill.
 *   card.hover       #34312C   text #E4DFD7 at 6% over bg.panel -- a project
 *                              card hovered. 1.164:1 above the card fill;
 *                              text.hi 11.378, text.dim 6.517, info 5.404.
 *   card.select      #3C2927   brand.crimson at 10% over bg.panel -- a project
 *                              card selected. text.hi 11.992, text.dim 6.869,
 *                              info 5.696, crimson border on it 3.392.
 * THE FILLS ARE PRE-COMPOSITED for the same reason bad.tint and warn.tint are:
 * a widget-level sheet outranks this one, so a control dressed from C++ has to
 * be able to name the identical literal. An rgba() would composite against
 * whatever happened to be underneath.
 * SEPARATION: brand.crimson is 16.0 degrees from bad #E86F62, which is the same
 * order this sheet already lives with between accent and warn (15.8), and 50.5
 * degrees from the accent. It is safe on this one window for a checkable
 * reason -- there is no destructive control on it -- and FORM does the rest: a
 * 72px full-width action card with a 40px filled tile and a halo is not
 * something a 28px inline danger button could be mistaken for.
 *
 * WORKED ARITHMETIC, so the method can be checked. Relative luminance: per
 * channel c/255, then c<=0.04045 ? c/12.92 : ((c+0.055)/1.055)^2.4;
 * L = 0.2126R + 0.7152G + 0.0722B. Ratio = (Lmax+0.05)/(Lmin+0.05).
 *   1) text #E4DFD7 -> L = 0.741756 ;  bg.panel #292621 -> L = 0.019674
 *      (0.741756+0.05)/(0.019674+0.05)                        = 11.3636:1
 *   2) bg.overlay #4A4640 -> L = 0.062063
 *      (0.741756+0.05)/(0.062063+0.05)                        =  7.0653:1
 *   3) ink #100E0B -> L = 0.004484 ;  accent #DFA52E -> L = 0.427955
 *      (0.427955+0.05)/(0.004484+0.05)                        =  8.7724:1
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * METRICS. One height, three radii, no gradients but the one that is data.
 * ===========================================================================
 *   control height   28px for QPushButton, QLineEdit, QComboBox and
 *                    QAbstractSpinBox. Three classes need three different
 *                    vertical paddings to land there. MEASURED on Qt 5.15.2,
 *                    Fusion, Segoe UI 12px, 1px border, with a standalone
 *                    probe that reports sizeHint and the laid-out height:
 *                      QPushButton   min-height 16 + padding 5px 12px   -> 28
 *                      QLineEdit     padding 1px 8px, min-height 16     -> 28
 *                      QAbstractSpinBox as QLineEdit, padding-right 20  -> 28
 *                      QComboBox     h = 19 + vpad_top + vpad_bottom, so
 *                                    padding 5px 24px 4px 10px          -> 28
 *                    The combo formula in the previous revision of this sheet
 *                    said h = 23 + vpad and would have produced 24px, one
 *                    control four pixels shorter than the button beside it.
 *                    Re-measure before changing any padding here.
 *   radii            The two tiers used to be 5px and 8px. That is a 1.6x
 *                    ratio, which does not read as two tiers at all, and 5px
 *                    on a 28px control is a 2015 radius. The set is now 2x.
 *                    CONTROL 6px (buttons, inputs, combos, spin boxes, tool
 *                    buttons, menu rows, list rows, the tab plate)
 *                    CONTAINER 12px (item-view wells, floating cards, the
 *                    project list, the minimap holder, text edits)
 *                    POPUP 0px (QMenu, combo popup, QToolTip) -- a rounded
 *                    top-level leaves the corner pixels showing the desktop on
 *                    Windows unless the popup carries WA_TranslucentBackground,
 *                    which is a C++ change in ApplicationEntry.cpp and not
 *                    this file's to make. THE QSS HALF MUST NOT SHIP ALONE or
 *                    four corner pixels of every menu show the desktop.
 *                    DERIVED INNER 5px = the 6px outer minus the 1px border,
 *                    on the spin-box steppers and the combo drop-down.
 *                    PILL, always exactly half the short side: 10px slider
 *                    handle (half of the 20px box), 3px slider groove (half of
 *                    6), 3px scroll thumb at rest and 4px hovered (half of 6
 *                    and of 8), 11px progress bar (half of 22) with a 10px
 *                    chunk (11 minus the 1px border).
 *                    INDICATOR 4px check box, UNCHANGED and not an oversight.
 *                    The drawn box is 13.5px, not 28, and the control tier is
 *                    a ratio: 6/28 = 0.214, and 0.214 x 13.5 = 2.9px, so 4px
 *                    is already the generous end of the same proportion where
 *                    5px would be 0.37 of the side. Radius follows proportion
 *                    at this size, not the tier number. The radio is a disc.
 *                    What DID change in the indicators is the ring colour --
 *                    see the specs table in generate_icons.py.
 *   border widths    1px on every control and hairline. 2px for a tab
 *                    underline and a list row leading rule. 3px for the rule
 *                    that marks the active tool and the dock title. Every
 *                    state RESERVES its rule as transparent, so becoming
 *                    :checked never reflows anything.
 *   QSS min-/max-    size the CONTENT rect, not the box. Measured: a
 *                    QToolButton with min-height 34px, padding 4px and a 1px
 *                    border lays out at 44px, not 34. The 34x34 tool buttons
 *                    below therefore declare the content size that ARRIVES at
 *                    34 after padding and borders, and the arithmetic is shown
 *                    at each rule.
 *
 * SPACING. One 4px scale; every margin, padding and gap comes from it.
 *   s0  2px   a state rule reserve; icon-to-label inside a compact control
 *   s1  4px   inside a UNIT: label to its control, chip to chip, grid gutters
 *   s2  6px   vertical padding in a dense row (menu item, list row, tab)
 *   s3  8px   DEFAULT gap between sibling controls; horizontal input padding;
 *             check box indicator to its text
 *   s4 12px   panel side margins; horizontal button padding; control to rule
 *   s5 16px   between SECTIONS in a panel; dialog content margins
 *   s6 24px   between major regions; dialog outer margins
 *   s7 32px   the column gutter on the project-selection window
 * Use the smallest step that still separates two things that are not part of
 * the same unit. Never an odd number; never a step that is not on this list.
 *
 * TYPE SCALE. Family is Segoe UI; nothing is bundled (see ATTRIBUTION.md).
 * Qt QSS has NO text-transform and NO letter-spacing, so every rank is
 * separable by SIZE and WEIGHT alone and colour only reinforces. No rank
 * shares both a size and a weight with any other.
 *   15/600 text.hi   window titles, the frameless title bar label
 *   14/700 text.hi   QDockWidget::title -- the app answer to "which tool am I
 *                    in", so it out-ranks every heading inside the panel
 *   13/700 text.hi   section headings: QGroupBox::title, QToolBox::tab,
 *                    ads--CDockWidgetTab
 *   12/400 text      the global default: labels, check boxes, buttons, tabs,
 *                    menu items
 *   12/600 text.hi   values: QLabel[state="value"], the selected tab, the
 *                    contents of an input, the viewport overlay label
 *   11/400 text.dim  secondary: captions, units, ranges, the status bar own
 *                    transient showMessage text
 *   11/700 text.dim  QHeaderView::section
 *   12 mono          QTextEdit / QPlainTextEdit -- reports, the Lua log, the
 *                    SQL changeset preview. Consolas; tabular figures matter.
 * GLOBAL SIZE STAYS 12px, which is what Qt 9pt Windows default resolves to at
 * 96 dpi, so the base does not resize and INTERFACE_FONT_PIXEL_SIZE in
 * ApplicationEntry.cpp is unchanged.
 * MEASURED LINE BOXES (QFontMetrics, Segoe UI, standalone probe): 11px -> 13,
 * 12px -> 15, 13px -> 17, 14px -> 19, 15px -> 20, 17px -> 23. Weight does not
 * change any of them. The two rules that had to move for the new sizes are
 * called out at QGroupBox and QDockWidget::title; nothing else reflows.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The Font Awesome icon font is NOT shipped with this fork. Nothing in this
 * sheet fixes a control size to a glyph, no state is signalled by an icon
 * alone, and the frameless title bar buttons get real PNGs via qproperty-icon
 * below -- so the window controls stay identifiable with no icon font at all.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * NOT REACHABLE FROM QSS -- for whoever wires the 3D side up in C++. Recorded
 * here so the viewport and the chrome stay one design. The accent moved, so
 * these move with it or the viewport still looks crimson.
 *   viewport.active     #DFA52E   accent -- the thing you are acting on
 *   viewport.select     #F0BA4A   accent.hi
 *   spawn.creature      #4FB87E   spawn.gameobject   #A98BE8
 *   waypoint.node/line  #DFA52E   (line at 60% alpha)
 *   chunk.ghost         #DFA52E   db.dirty  #E2803C   db.deleted #E86F62 @40%
 *   layer budget 1-2    #4FB87E @25%   3  #E2803C @30%   4  #E86F62 @35%
 * ======================================================================== */


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- global -- */

QWidget {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: none;
	font-family: "Segoe UI";
	font-size: 12px;
	outline: 0;
	selection-background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.34);
	selection-color: #F3F0E9;
}

QWidget:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QDialog {
	background-color: #292621;
}

QMessageBox {
	background-color: #292621;
}

QMessageBox QLabel {
	color: #E4DFD7;
	font-size: 12px;
}

QLabel {
	background: transparent;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	padding: 1px 0px;
}

QLabel:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

/* A hyperlink is information, not an action, so it takes info and not the
   accent -- the accent means "the thing you are acting on" and nothing else.
   info on bg.panel is 6.29:1. */
QLabel[openExternalLinks="true"] {
	color: #6FAEDC;
}

/* QFrame is a base class, so a bare QFrame rule would repaint every scroll
   area, tree, list and text edit in the app. Only the two line shapes are
   targeted here. */
QFrame[frameShape="4"] {
	background-color: #403B35;
	border: none;
	max-height: 1px;
}

QFrame[frameShape="5"] {
	background-color: #403B35;
	border: none;
	max-width: 1px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------- window, docks, gutters -- */

/* The window recedes. bg.void is the floor everything else sits on and it is
   what surrounds the 3D viewport: 2.057:1 below the floating cards drawn over
   the render, so a card never washes out against snowy Dun Morogh. */
QMainWindow {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}

QMainWindow::separator {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	width: 4px;
	height: 4px;
}

/* THE HOVER IS edge, NOT bg.raised. A splitter is a drag affordance drawn on
   the bg.void gutter, and bg.raised measures 1.638:1 there -- under the 3:1
   floor, on the one cue that tells you the gutter can be dragged at all. edge
   is 5.138:1 and the press already goes accent at 8.772:1. (This figure was
   published as 2.409:1. No pair in this palette yields that; the sheet's own
   surface ladder gives it directly -- bg.void -> bg.panel 1.279 times
   bg.panel -> bg.raised 1.281 is 1.638. The conclusion survives the
   correction, and survives it by more than it used to.)

   NOT DONE, and recorded rather than guessed at: the two grip PNGs
   (handle_vertical.png, handle_horizontal.png) are 2x assets 24px along their
   long axis, and this handle is 4px across. Dropping a 24px grip mark into a
   4px band is a scaling question this sheet cannot answer without running the
   editor, so the handle stays a plain bar and the whole affordance is carried
   by the colour step. */
QMainWindow::separator:hover {
	background-color: #8A8378;
}

QSplitter {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QSplitter::handle {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}

QSplitter::handle:horizontal {
	width: 4px;
}

QSplitter::handle:vertical {
	height: 4px;
}

QSplitter::handle:hover {
	background-color: #8A8378;
}

QSplitter::handle:pressed {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
}

QDockWidget {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	font-size: 14px;
	font-weight: 700;
	border: none;
	titlebar-close-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close.png);
	titlebar-normal-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_restore.png);
}

/* MapView retitles this dock from the active tool on every mode change, so
   this line is the application answer to "which tool am I in" and it has to
   out-rank every heading inside the panel: 14/700 text.hi at 13.24:1 against
   the panel, against 13/700 for a section heading and 12/400 for a label.

   The rule is on the LEFT because a dock title is a horizontal band whose left
   edge is where the eye starts, and a bottom rule would collide with the
   hairline separating the title from the panel.

   REFLOW: the size went 13px -> 14px, whose line box measures 19px against 17.
   Padding drops from 8px/7px to 7px/6px in the same edit, so the bar stays
   19+7+6 = 32 plus the hairline, the height it has always been, and no dock
   moves. */
QDockWidget::title {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: none;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #8A8378;
	padding: 7px 8px 6px 10px;
	font-size: 14px;
	font-weight: 700;
	text-align: left;
}

QDockWidget::close-button,
QDockWidget::float-button {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 6px;
	width: 22px;
	height: 22px;
	padding: 0px;
	icon-size: 12px;
}

QDockWidget::close-button:hover,
QDockWidget::float-button:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
}

QDockWidget::close-button:pressed,
QDockWidget::float-button:pressed {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------- menus, menu bar -- */

/* The menu bar is part of the window frame, so it sits on bg.void. A selected
   item therefore LIFTS to bg.raised (1.281:1) -- the bar is already the floor
   and there is nothing below it to recess into. */
QMenuBar {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
	padding: 3px 8px;
	spacing: 2px;
}

QMenuBar::item {
	background: transparent;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	padding: 6px 10px;
	margin: 0px 0px;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 6px;
}

QMenuBar::item:selected {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* The menu is open: accent at 0.24 over bg.void composites to #423213, a
   1.558:1 plate, with text.hi on it at 10.87:1. */
QMenuBar::item:pressed {
	background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QMenuBar::item:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

/* SQUARE CORNERS, AND THIS IS THE ONE PLACE THE MODERNISATION STOPS. A
   rounded top-level popup leaves the four corner pixels showing whatever is
   behind the window on Windows, because the popup is its own native window and
   the style sheet cannot punch a hole in it. The fix is not a QSS one: the
   popup needs Qt::WA_TranslucentBackground, set from an eventFilter on qApp
   for QEvent::Show, or from QProxyStyle::polish(QWidget*), next to where the
   sheet is installed in ApplicationEntry.cpp. That is a different file and a
   different owner.

   SO THE RADIUS STAYS 0 HERE UNTIL THAT LANDS. Shipping the QSS half alone
   would trade a dated corner for a visibly broken one, which is a worse
   outcome than the thing it was trying to fix. The same applies to the combo
   popup and to QToolTip further down; all three are marked. */
QMenu {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 6px;
}

/* Padding went 5px/4px to a uniform 6px and the row margin went 1px 3px to
   1px 0px. The total horizontal inset barely moves (4 + 3 = 7 against
   6 + 0 = 6) but all of it now belongs to the MENU, so the highlight plate
   runs the full width of the popup instead of floating three pixels inside it.

   The right padding is 24px and not 30px. Thirty was a shortcut column that
   most items in this application do not have, so every menu was six pixels
   wider than its widest item for nothing; 24px still clears the 5px submenu
   chevron and its 10px margin. THE LEFT STAYS 30px because the selected rule
   pays for its 2px accent leading rule out of the padding -- 2 + 28 = 30 --
   and that arithmetic is what stops a menu row shifting sideways when you
   point at it. */
QMenu::item {
	background: transparent;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	padding: 6px 24px 6px 30px;
	margin: 1px 0px;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 6px;
}

/* A menu item has no :hover -- Qt marks the item under the cursor :selected --
   so this one rule carries both meanings.

   SELECTION HERE RECESSES rather than tinting, and that is measured rather
   than stylistic: no accent alpha over bg.overlay keeps body text at 7:1
   (0.16 alpha gives 5.49:1, 0.24 gives 4.81:1). Dropping to bg.raised gives
   body text 8.87:1, and the 2px accent left rule reads at 5.36:1 against it.
   This is the one surface in the sheet where selection goes DOWN. */
QMenu::item:selected {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	padding-left: 28px;
}

QMenu::item:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #7F786A;
}

/* stroke.hi, not stroke: on bg.overlay stroke measures 1.18:1 and is simply
   not there. stroke.hi is 1.84:1. */
QMenu::separator {
	height: 1px;
	background-color: #746D64;
	margin: 5px 12px;
}

QMenu::indicator {
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
	left: 9px;
}

QMenu::indicator:non-exclusive:checked,
QMenu::indicator:exclusive:checked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_menu_check.png);
}

QMenu::indicator:non-exclusive:checked:disabled,
QMenu::indicator:exclusive:checked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_menu_check_disabled.png);
}

QMenu::right-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/right_arrow.png);
	width: 5px;
	height: 8px;
	margin-right: 10px;
}

QMenu::right-arrow:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/right_arrow_disabled.png);
}

QMenu::scroller,
QMenu::tearoff {
	background-color: #4A4640;
}


/* --------------------------------------------------------------- toolbar -- */

/* The DOCKED toolbar. bg.raised, one hairline lid. The floating cards drawn
   over the 3D view are a different thing entirely and are styled at the end of
   this file. */
/* The docked toolbar takes the same one-pixel bevel the button does, for the
   same measured reason: edge.lit on this bg.raised fill is 4.648:1 while the
   stroke.soft lid below it is 1.06:1, so until now the bar had no top edge at
   all against the menu bar above it.

   NO REFLOW. min-height sizes the CONTENT rect, so the box was
   36 + 4 + 4 + 0 (no top border) + 1 (lid) = 45. It is now
   35 + 4 + 4 + 1 + 1 = 45. The bar is the height it has always been. */
QToolBar {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #A9A296;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
	spacing: 4px;
	padding: 4px;
	min-height: 35px;
}

QToolBar:vertical {
	border-bottom: none;
	border-right: 1px solid #403B35;
	min-height: 0px;
}

/* edge, not stroke: on the bg.raised bar stroke measures 1.479:1, and a
   separator that cannot be found is not a separator. edge is 3.137:1. */
QToolBar::separator {
	background-color: #8A8378;
	width: 1px;
	height: 1px;
	margin: 5px 4px;
}

QToolBar::handle:horizontal {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/handle_vertical.png);
	width: 8px;
	margin: 2px 1px;
}

QToolBar::handle:vertical {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/handle_horizontal.png);
	height: 8px;
	margin: 1px 2px;
}


/* ----------------------------------------------------------- status bar -- */

/* Seven readouts, every one a bare QLabel with no object name -- MapView
   builds _status_position, _status_selection, _status_area, _status_time,
   _status_fps, _status_culling and _status_database and hands each straight to
   addWidget. This sheet cannot make one out-rank another; what it can do is
   stop them running together, with a hairline before each field. QSS has no
   :first-child, so the leftmost field also gets a leading rule; it lands
   against the window edge and reads as part of the frame.

   The readouts are text on bg.void (14.53:1); the transient showMessage text
   QStatusBar paints itself stays at 11/400 text.dim (9.70:1), so a five-second
   "Map saved" no longer looks louder than the position.

   Per-field emphasis needs one line of C++ each: QLabel[state="value"] below
   is the mechanism and it carries an attribute selector, so it outranks the
   plain descendant rule here. Set it once at construction on FPS, position and
   the loaded/rendered counts and they lift to 12/600 text.hi. */
QStatusBar {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #403B35;
	font-size: 11px;
	min-height: 26px;
	padding: 0px 2px;
}

QStatusBar::item {
	border: none;
}

/* The field rule is edge and not stroke.soft. Seven readouts sit in one bar
   with nothing between them but a hairline, and stroke.soft on bg.void is
   1.740:1 -- which is why they run together into a single sentence. edge is
   5.138:1 there. This is a separator between two pieces of INFORMATION, so it
   carries the 3:1 graphical floor rather than the 1.4:1 a seam gets away
   with. */
QStatusBar QLabel {
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border-left: 1px solid #8A8378;
	font-size: 11px;
	padding: 2px 10px;
}

QStatusBar QLabel:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

/* THE EIGHT OBJECT NAMES THE C++ ALREADY MINTS. NoggitWindow::prepareStatusBar
   names the bar "editor-status-bar" and dressStatusReadout names the seven
   readouts positionally from STATUS_READOUT_NAMES, and until now this sheet
   used none of them -- eight hooks were cut for a lock that was never fitted.
   Two of the seven are worth a rule.

   THE LEFTMOST FIELD LOSES ITS LEADING RULE. The paragraph above concedes the
   defect and cannot fix it, because QSS has no :first-child: the hairline that
   separates field n from field n-1 is drawn by every field including the one
   with nothing to its left, so the position readout carried an edge hairline
   1px from the window's own frame. An id pair scores (1 + 0x100) + (1 + 0x100)
   = 514 against "QStatusBar QLabel"'s 2, so this reaches it by cascade rather
   than by document order. The 10px the text used to sit inside becomes 12px,
   so it does not slide left by the pixel the border gave back.

   THE DATABASE FIELD IS CONTEXT, NOT DATA. It is written once, at map load and
   only under USE_MYSQL_UID_STORAGE, and never updates again. Position, FPS and
   the tile counts change every frame and already lift to the 12/600 text.hi
   value rank through QLabel[state="value"]. A never-changing configuration
   note sharing the 11/400 text rank with them was the loudest thing in the bar
   that had nothing to say. text.dim on bg.void is 9.699:1, still over the 7:1
   prose floor this sheet sets for itself, and it is now quieter than the
   readouts beside it rather than equal to them.

   NO min-width RESERVE, and that is a refusal rather than an oversight. The
   obvious cure for a bar that twitches once a second is to pin the two live
   counters to a fixed width, but a reserve has to be at least as wide as the
   longest string it reserves for, and the strings MapView builds are long --
   the FPS field is "FPS: %1 - Average frame time: %2ms" and the culling field
   names four counts in one label. At that length the reserve is most of the
   bar. The twitch is real; its fix is a shorter string in MapView, not a width
   here. */
QStatusBar#editor-status-bar QLabel#status-position {
	border-left: none;
	padding-left: 12px;
}

QLabel#status-database {
	color: #BFB7AA;
}

QSizeGrip {
	background: transparent;
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/sizegrip.png);
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
}


/* --------------------------------------------------------------- buttons -- */

/* A button COMES FORWARD (bg.raised) and an input GOES BACK (bg.void). That
   one difference is what stops "press this" and "type here" looking like the
   same object, and it is the reason the previous sheet -- where both sat on
   bg.raised with the same border -- read as a wireframe.

   HOVER LIFTS, PRESS RECESSES. The press drops two whole surface levels to
   bg.void, which is unmistakable against a hover that went up one.

   Geometry is identical in all five states: a 1px border on all four sides
   everywhere, so nothing reflows. Measured height 28px (probe: sizeHint
   65x28), and that measurement still holds -- no padding and no border WIDTH
   changed in this revision, only border colours and the corner radius.

   THE EDGE, AND WHY THIS IS THE CHANGE THAT MAKES THE SHEET VISIBLE AT ALL. A
   button used to be identified by three cues and every one of them sat under
   the 3:1 floor: its border #565049 against its own fill 1.479:1, that border
   against the bg.panel around it 1.894:1, and its fill against that panel
   1.281:1. Three invisible cues is a wireframe. The border is now edge
   #8A8378: 3.137:1 against the fill and 4.018:1 against the panel.

   AND IT IS A BEVEL, not a flat ring. One pixel, three tones, lit from above:
     top    edge.lit #A9A296   4.648:1 vs the fill, 5.953:1 vs the panel
     sides  edge     #8A8378   3.137:1 vs the fill
     bottom stroke   #565049   1.479:1 vs the fill -- deliberately grounded
   Top against bottom is 3.143:1, so the bevel is a step you can see and not
   the usual 1.05:1 rumour. A gradient fill was measured and rejected:
   #474239 -> #38332D drops the edge contrast to 2.658:1 at the top stop, i.e.
   a gradient buys its depth by SPENDING the edge contrast that was the whole
   point of the exercise.

   HOVER drops the bevel for a single bright ring, text.dim #BFB7AA, 4.716:1
   on the bg.overlay hover fill and 1.888:1 above the resting edge. stroke.hi
   cannot serve here: at 2.305:1 on bg.raised it is DARKER than the resting
   edge, so the old hover was a recess pretending to be a lift.

   PRESSED INVERTS THE BEVEL -- lit on the bottom, grounded on top -- so the
   press is felt as a physical inset and not only as a change of fill. On the
   bg.void pressed fill edge.lit reads 7.613:1 and stroke reads 2.422:1. */
QPushButton {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border-top: 1px solid #A9A296;
	border-right: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #565049;
	border-left: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 6px;
	padding: 5px 12px;
	min-height: 16px;
}

QPushButton:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	border: 1px solid #BFB7AA;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton:pressed {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border-top: 1px solid #565049;
	border-right: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #A9A296;
	border-left: 1px solid #8A8378;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton:checked {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* Focus is a border colour change and nothing else -- no glow, no outline, no
   size change, so nothing reflows when focus moves.

   CHECKED, AND WHY THERE IS NO QPushButton:checked:focus HERE. QToolButton
   needs one: its :focus rule restates the 3px leading reserve as transparent
   and therefore erases the accent bar :checked draws, at equal specificity and
   later in document order. This family does NOT have that shape, and it was
   checked rather than assumed: QPushButton:focus declares one property, the
   same `1px solid #DFA52E` border that QPushButton:checked already declares,
   so a checked button that takes focus loses nothing -- the fill (#100E0B) and
   the colour (text.hi) come from :checked and are not restated here. A
   :checked:focus rule would be a rule with nothing to say.

   HONEST LIMIT, recorded because it is real and it is NOT this file's to fix
   alone: a checked-unfocused button and an unchecked-FOCUSED one therefore
   carry the identical accent ring and differ only by fill, bg.void #100E0B
   against bg.raised #3C3732, which is 1.638:1 -- and by text rank, text.hi
   against text, 1.165:1. Neither separates them. It costs nothing while
   :checked is rare on a push button, but FlattenTool's four flatten modes are
   now checkable QPushButtons in a QButtonGroup rather than radio buttons, so
   the collision is on screen. The fix is a distinct checked FACE (the gizmo
   bar's `color: #DFA52E` on the bg.void face, 8.772:1, is the pattern already
   in this file) and it belongs in the same pass that decides whether those
   chips stay push buttons at all. */
QPushButton:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
}

QPushButton:default {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton:disabled {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QPushButton:flat {
	background: transparent;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
}

QPushButton:flat:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
}

QPushButton:flat:pressed {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton::menu-indicator {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
	subcontrol-origin: padding;
	subcontrol-position: center right;
	right: 6px;
}

QDialogButtonBox QPushButton {
	min-width: 88px;
	padding: 5px 16px;
}

/* Destructive actions. Set from C++ with  btn->setProperty("state", "danger");
   and, if the button already existed, style()->unpolish(btn); polish(btn);

   THE RED IS THE EDGE, NOT THE TEXT, and that is measured: bad #E86F62 as text
   on bg.raised is 3.86:1 and fails the body floor. As a 1px border it clears
   the 3:1 non-text floor, and the label stays text at 8.87:1. Hover is the
   state that commits to the fill, where ink on bad is 6.31:1.

   The press used to darken to #C4574A, which put ink at 4.41:1 -- below the
   4.5 floor, on a control that is ENABLED, so nothing exempted it. The fill
   is now bad.press #C85F54: ink 4.79:1, still 1.32:1 darker than the hover
   fill it steps down from, and 3.75:1 against the bg.panel dialog body. */
QPushButton[state="danger"] {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid #E86F62;
}

QPushButton[state="danger"]:hover {
	background-color: #E86F62;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #E86F62;
}

QPushButton[state="danger"]:pressed {
	background-color: #C85F54;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #C85F54;
}

/* The confirming action in a two-button row. Set from C++ the same way danger
   is:  btn->setProperty("state", "primary");  and unpolish/polish if the
   button already existed. At most one per window, marking the one action the
   window exists to perform.

   Ink on the fill, not white: #100E0B on #DFA52E is 8.77:1. Gold also removes
   the special case crimson needed -- ink on accent.press is 5.64:1, so the
   press no longer has to invert to light text.

   :disabled is spelled out with two conditions because a bare
   QPushButton:disabled ties with a one-attribute rule on Qt specificity and
   would then be decided by document order alone. Qt scores an element name +1
   and a pseudo-class and an attribute selector +0x10 each, so both are
   1 + 0x10 = 17 -- the figures published here were 257, which is the W3C a/b/c
   number and not what QCss::Selector::specificity() returns. Every specificity
   figure in this file is now Qt's; the model, and where it lives in the Qt
   source, is set out in full at QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider. The tie
   is a tie under either model, so the rule below does not change. */
QPushButton[state="primary"] {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	font-weight: 600;
}

QPushButton[state="primary"]:hover {
	background-color: #F0BA4A;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #F0BA4A;
}

QPushButton[state="primary"]:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
}

QPushButton[state="primary"]:focus {
	border: 1px solid #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton[state="primary"]:disabled {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
}


/* ----------------------------------------------------------- tool buttons -- */

/* The base rule for every QToolButton in the application. HOVER LIFTS, ACTIVE
   RECESSES: the two can never be confused because they move in opposite
   directions. On the default bg.raised surface a hover goes up to bg.overlay
   and a checked button drops to bg.void, a 1.638:1 recess, with a 3px accent
   leading rule at 5.36:1 and a gold glyph.

   Both the 1px border and the 3px border-left are declared in every state at
   those exact widths, so switching tools cannot reflow a bar. Only colours
   move.

   NOTE ON SPECIFICITY, carried forward and still true: an ancestor
   pseudo-state (QToolBar:horizontal QToolButton) is parsed and silently
   DISCARDED by Qt -- QCss::StyleSelector exposes virtuals for a node name, ids
   and attributes but none for its pseudo-state. Scope by object name, class
   name or attribute only. check_qss.py fails the sheet if one reappears. */
/* DENSITY. This rule used to lay out at 1 + 4 + 24 + 4 + 1 = 34px while the
   QPushButton beside it measured 28, so wherever the two families share a row
   they stood six pixels apart. padding 4px -> 3px and min-height 24 -> 20 give
   1 + 3 + 20 + 3 + 1 = 28 and the two families finally line up. A 16px icon
   still fits the 20px content rect with two pixels to spare.

   This does NOT reach the left tool strip or the view-toggle bar: both restate
   min-height, max-height and padding explicitly further down and their 34x34
   arithmetic is deliberate. */
QToolButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 6px;
	padding: 3px 6px;
	min-width: 24px;
	min-height: 20px;
}

QToolButton:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
}

QToolButton:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-left: 3px solid #B8801F;
}

QToolButton:checked {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

QToolButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* Focus was missing on every QToolButton in the application. QWidget sets
   outline: 0 at the top of this sheet, which suppresses the focus rect Qt
   would otherwise draw, and nothing replaced it here. accent on the surfaces a
   tool button rests on measures 6.860:1 (panel), 5.356:1 (raised) and 4.265:1
   (overlay), all clear of the 3:1 floor for a non-text state mark.

   It sits here, after the pointer states and before :disabled, exactly where
   QPushButton:focus sits, so the two families resolve ties the same way: equal
   specificity, later rule wins, so focus out-ranks hover on the BORDER while
   hover keeps its fill. The 3px leading reserve is RESTATED, or the shorthand
   collapses it to 1px and the bar reflows the moment you tab into it. */
QToolButton:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
}

/* AND THIS ONE IS NOT OPTIONAL, IT REPAIRS THE RULE ABOVE. Restating the 3px
   reserve as TRANSPARENT is what stops the ring reflowing the row -- but it
   also erases the accent leading bar that QToolButton:checked draws, because
   the two rules carry one pseudo-class each and therefore tie on Qt
   specificity (1 + 0x10 = 17 apiece, the model is named in full at
   QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider) and :focus is later in document order.
   Without this rule every checked tool button in the application loses its
   "this mode is on" cue the instant it takes keyboard focus, and is then
   distinguishable from a focused UNCHECKED one by fill alone -- bg.void
   #100E0B against a transparent face, which over the bg.panel a tool button
   normally sits on is 1.279:1 and not a cue at all.

   The two scoped bars further down already carried their own :checked:focus
   for exactly this reason; what was missing was the BASE. Two pseudo-classes
   is 1 + 0x20 = 33, so this out-ranks both :checked and :focus outright and
   the result no longer depends on where it sits. Checked AND focused therefore
   keeps the accent bar at 8.772:1 on its own bg.void face AND shows the accent
   ring, which is 6.860:1 on bg.panel, 5.356:1 on bg.raised and 4.265:1 on
   bg.overlay -- all clear of the 3:1 floor SC 2.4.13 asks of a focus mark.

   BORDER ONLY, DELIBERATELY. Restating background-color here would also beat
   QToolButton:checked:hover, which is the other two-pseudo rule and therefore
   ties at 33: this rule is later, so a checked button that is BOTH focused and
   hovered would drop from the #1D1916 hover face back to #100E0B and lose its
   pointer response. Declaring only the two border sides lets the fill keep
   cascading from :checked / :checked:hover while the ring and the bar come
   from here. */
QToolButton:checked:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

QToolButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
}

QToolButton[popupMode="1"] {
	padding-right: 18px;
}

QToolButton::menu-button {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #8A8378;
	width: 14px;
}

QToolButton::menu-button:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
}

QToolButton::menu-arrow,
QToolButton::menu-indicator {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QToolButton::menu-arrow:disabled,
QToolButton::menu-indicator:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow_disabled.png);
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- inputs -- */

/* A FIELD IS A WELL. It goes back to bg.void while a button comes forward to
   bg.raised; that is how the two stop looking alike. 28px tall, 6px radius,
   1px edge at 5.138:1 against the fill, contents in text.hi at 16.93:1.

   THE EDGE MOVED, for the reason spelled out at QPushButton: the old 1px
   stroke #565049 measured 2.422:1 against this bg.void fill and 1.894:1
   against the bg.panel the field sits on -- the second of those is what
   decides whether you can find the field at all, and it was under the floor.
   edge #8A8378 is 5.138:1 and 4.018:1. Hover follows the button and goes to
   text.dim #BFB7AA, 9.699:1 on the fill and 1.888:1 above the resting edge;
   stroke.hi was 2.952:1 on panel and could not carry it. Focus is unchanged
   and still accent. A field gets no bevel: a well is not a raised thing.

   Focus is TWO cues and no reflow: the fill lifts one hair to bg.alt and the
   border turns accent (8.77:1 against the fill). A focused field is NEVER
   tinted -- the tint is reserved for warn and error, and it is what separates
   an orange-bordered field from a gold-bordered one when the two hues are only
   15.8 degrees apart.

   MEASURED heights (Qt 5.15.2 probe, Fusion, Segoe UI 12px, 1px border):
     QLineEdit        padding 1px 8px, min-height 16   -> 28
     QAbstractSpinBox as above plus padding-right 20   -> 28
     QComboBox        h = 19 + vpad_top + vpad_bottom  -> 5px/4px lands on 28
   Change a padding here and you have changed the height; re-measure. */
QLineEdit,
QTextEdit,
QPlainTextEdit,
QAbstractSpinBox,
QComboBox {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 6px;
	font-weight: 600;
	selection-background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.34);
	selection-color: #F3F0E9;
}

QLineEdit,
QAbstractSpinBox {
	padding: 1px 8px;
	min-height: 16px;
}

QLineEdit:hover,
QTextEdit:hover,
QPlainTextEdit:hover,
QAbstractSpinBox:hover,
QComboBox:hover {
	border: 1px solid #BFB7AA;
}

QLineEdit:focus,
QTextEdit:focus,
QPlainTextEdit:focus,
QAbstractSpinBox:focus,
QComboBox:focus {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
}

QLineEdit:disabled,
QTextEdit:disabled,
QPlainTextEdit:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox:disabled,
QComboBox:disabled {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QLineEdit:read-only {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
	font-weight: 400;
}

/* Reports, the Lua console log and any SQL preview want tabular figures. They
   are containers rather than single-line controls, so they take the 8px
   container radius and body weight. */
QTextEdit,
QPlainTextEdit {
	font-family: "Consolas";
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: 400;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border-radius: 12px;
	padding: 8px;
}

/* Error and warning states on an input. Set from C++ with
   setProperty("state", "error") / ("state", "warn") plus unpolish/polish.

   THE TINT IS LOAD-BEARING. Border alone is not enough: warn #E2803C and the
   accent #DFA52E are 15.8 degrees apart, so a bordered-only warn field and a
   focused field would be told apart by hue alone. The 14%-alpha fill over
   bg.void is a difference in KIND. Composited: bad -> #2E1C17, warn ->
   #2D1E12; the border reads 5.32:1 and 5.66:1 against its own fill and the
   contents stay at 14.25:1 and 14.13:1. */
QLineEdit[state="error"],
QAbstractSpinBox[state="error"],
QComboBox[state="error"] {
	background-color: #2E1C17;
	border: 1px solid #E86F62;
}

QLineEdit[state="warn"],
QAbstractSpinBox[state="warn"],
QComboBox[state="warn"] {
	background-color: #2D1E12;
	border: 1px solid #E2803C;
}

QLabel[state="error"] {
	color: #E86F62;
}

QLabel[state="warn"] {
	color: #E2803C;
}

QLabel[state="ok"] {
	color: #4FB87E;
}

/* A label carrying a live value -- the status bar readouts, the current
   radius, a spawn GUID. The "value" rank: 12/600 text.hi. */
QLabel[state="value"] {
	color: #F3F0E9;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: 600;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------ spin boxes -- */

/* The steppers are a strip inside the well rather than two chiclets bolted to
   it: no fill of their own until hovered, one 1px divider. */
QAbstractSpinBox {
	padding-right: 20px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button {
	subcontrol-origin: border;
	subcontrol-position: top right;
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-top-right-radius: 5px;
	width: 17px;
	margin: 1px 1px 0px 0px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::down-button {
	subcontrol-origin: border;
	subcontrol-position: bottom right;
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-bottom-right-radius: 5px;
	width: 17px;
	margin: 0px 1px 1px 0px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button:hover,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-button:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button:pressed,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-button:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-button:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::down-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-arrow:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox::up-arrow:off {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow_disabled.png);
}

QAbstractSpinBox::down-arrow:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-arrow:off {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow_disabled.png);
}


/* -------------------------------------------------------------- combo box -- */

/* 5px top / 4px bottom is measured, not chosen: a combo lays out at
   19 + vpad_top + vpad_bottom, so 9px of vertical padding is the only way to
   land on the same 28px as the button beside it. A sweep of every pair from
   3/2 to 7/6 was run through the probe; 3/2 -- what the design note asked for
   -- produces 24px. */
QComboBox {
	padding: 5px 24px 4px 10px;
	min-height: 16px;
}

QComboBox:on {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
}

QComboBox::drop-down {
	subcontrol-origin: border;
	subcontrol-position: top right;
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-top-right-radius: 5px;
	border-bottom-right-radius: 5px;
	width: 20px;
	margin: 1px;
}

QComboBox::drop-down:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
}

QComboBox::drop-down:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QComboBox::down-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QComboBox::down-arrow:on {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow.png);
}

QComboBox::down-arrow:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow_disabled.png);
}

/* The popup is a separate top-level widget and does not inherit the combo own
   rule, so it has to be styled by name. It is a popup: bg.overlay, stroke.hi,
   square corners. Selection RECESSES to bg.raised for the same measured reason
   the menu does -- no accent alpha over bg.overlay keeps body text at 7:1.
   Square corners for the reason spelled out at QMenu: it is a top-level popup
   and rounding it needs WA_TranslucentBackground from C++ first. */
QComboBox QAbstractItemView {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 4px;
	outline: 0;
	selection-background-color: #3C3732;
	selection-color: #F3F0E9;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------- check boxes and radios -- */

/* The nine-radio brush type grid is the most prominent control in the editor.
   The indicator is a 16px well with a 2px stroke ring, flooded accent with an
   ink glyph when checked. All artwork is generated PNG: change the constants
   in generate_icons.py and re-run it, never hand-draw.

   Label colour follows the four ranks: text at rest, text.hi on hover and when
   checked, text.off when disabled. */
/* THE BORDER IS A FOCUS RESERVE AND IS TRANSPARENT AT REST. These two had no
   border at all, so there was nowhere for a focus ring to go and adding one on
   :focus alone would have grown the row by two pixels the instant you tabbed
   into it. Reserving it costs nothing vertically -- padding 3 + 3 becomes
   border 1 + padding 2, twice, so the box is the height it always was -- and
   two pixels horizontally, which no layout in this application depends on. */
QCheckBox,
QRadioButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	spacing: 8px;
	padding: 2px 0px;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 6px;
}

QCheckBox:hover,
QRadioButton:hover {
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QCheckBox:checked,
QRadioButton:checked {
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* Placed after :hover and :checked and before :disabled, the same order
   QPushButton and QToolButton use, so a hovered focused box shows the hover
   colour AND the ring. accent against the bg.panel these sit on is 6.860:1,
   over the 3:1 floor for a non-text state mark. The nine-radio brush grid is
   the most keyboard-navigated control in the editor and until now arrowing
   through it changed nothing you could see. */
QCheckBox:focus,
QRadioButton:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
}

QCheckBox:disabled,
QRadioButton:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QCheckBox::indicator,
QRadioButton::indicator,
QGroupBox::indicator {
	width: 16px;
	height: 16px;
}

QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_unchecked.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked:hover,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_unchecked_hover.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked:disabled,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_unchecked_disabled.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_checked.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked:hover,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_checked_hover.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked:disabled,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_checked_disabled.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:indeterminate {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_indeterminate.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:indeterminate:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_indeterminate_disabled.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:unchecked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_unchecked.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:unchecked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_unchecked_hover.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:unchecked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_unchecked_disabled.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:checked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_checked.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:checked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_checked_hover.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:checked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_checked_disabled.png);
}


/* -------------------------------------------------------------- group box -- */

/* THIS IS NO LONGER THE RULE THE TOOL DOCK USES. Read it with "the tool dock"
   further down this file, which re-scopes every declaration below to
   Noggit--Ui--Tools--ToolPanel and draws a section there as a bg.panel card on
   a bg.void ground instead. What is left here reaches the group boxes in the
   settings window, the dialogs and the wizards, where a heading over a rule is
   still the right construction because those forms sit on bg.panel already and
   have nowhere to recess to.

   Stock Qt draws a group box as a bordered rectangle with a centred bold
   underlined title, so a form built out of them reads as a stack of 1998
   dialog frames. Here there is no box: the title is a heading in the widget
   own top margin, one rule runs under it, and that rule is the only mark.

   THE RULE IS edge #8A8378. It was stroke #565049, which measures 1.894:1 on
   bg.panel, and the argument for it was that this is the quietest line that
   can still be found. Re-measured, it cannot: 1.894:1 is under the 3:1 floor
   WCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.11 sets for a graphical object, and this is not a decorative
   seam -- it is the application primary structural divider. edge is 4.018:1 on
   bg.panel, which is the quietest line that can ACTUALLY be found.
   stroke.soft, at 1.360:1, was never a candidate.

   REFLOW, stated so nobody is surprised: the title goes 11/700 -> 13/700, the
   section-heading rank. Its line box measures 17px (probe: QFontMetrics, Segoe
   UI 13px, any weight). margin-top therefore goes 20px -> 22px and the title
   padding-bottom 6px -> 5px, so 17 + 5 = 22 fits the margin exactly. Every
   group section grows by 2px and nothing else moves. (The dock's own sections
   went BACK to 11/700 when they became cards -- the heading rank the two brand
   windows use -- and their margin to 21px with it. The paragraph above is the
   history of this rule, not of that one.) */
QGroupBox {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 0px;
	margin-top: 22px;
	margin-bottom: 4px;
	padding: 12px 0px 12px 0px;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 700;
	spacing: 8px;
}

QGroupBox::title {
	subcontrol-origin: margin;
	subcontrol-position: top left;
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	left: 0px;
	padding: 0px 8px 5px 0px;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 700;
}

QGroupBox::title:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

/* A disabled section drops one step down the stroke ramp. */
QGroupBox:disabled {
	border-top: 1px solid #403B35;
}

/* A group box nested inside another one gets no rule of its own, or the panel
   turns into a ladder. The tool dock's scoped twin does the same thing for the
   same reason, one specificity point higher so it wins on cascade rather than
   on document order. */
QGroupBox QGroupBox {
	border-top: none;
	margin-top: 16px;
	padding-top: 4px;
}

/* A checkable group box needs its indicator clear of the title text. */
QGroupBox::indicator {
	margin-right: 6px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- tabs -- */

/* THE HOVER USED TO BE LOUDER THAN THE SELECTION, which is the single worst
   ordering defect in this file. An inactive tab under the pointer got a full
   bg.raised plate with rounded top corners; the SELECTED tab got
   `background: transparent` and a 2px underline. A tab you were merely
   pointing at therefore looked more current than the tab you were actually
   on. Worse, the two rules carried one pseudo-state each -- equal specificity,
   decided by document order alone -- and :selected came second, so the
   selected tab had no hover response whatsoever.

   Rebuilt so the two cues run in the right direction and the volume matches
   the meaning:
     HOVER     text at 5% alpha over whatever is behind the strip. Over
               bg.panel that composites to #322F2A, a 1.131:1 lift carrying
               text.hi at 11.710:1 -- a wash, not a plate.
     SELECTED  the bg.raised plate the hover used to steal, 1.281:1 over
               bg.panel with text.hi at 10.339:1, PLUS the 2px accent underline
               at 5.356:1 against that plate, PLUS the 12/600 value rank. The
               front tab now sits on a surface and continues into the pane.
     BOTH      bg.overlay, 1.256:1 above the selected plate, accent underline
               held. Two pseudo-states, so it out-ranks both single-state rules
               on specificity and no longer depends on document order.
   Order matters and is deliberate: hover, then selected, then the compound.
   The 2px rule is still reserved as transparent at rest, so selecting a tab
   moves nothing. */
QTabWidget::pane {
	background-color: #292621;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #403B35;
	border-radius: 0px;
	top: -1px;
}

QTabWidget::tab-bar {
	left: 0px;
}

QTabBar {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	qproperty-drawBase: 0;
}

QTabBar::tab {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
	padding: 8px 16px;
	min-width: 52px;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: 400;
}

QTabBar::tab:hover {
	background-color: rgba(228, 223, 215, 0.05);
	border-top-left-radius: 6px;
	border-top-right-radius: 6px;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QTabBar::tab:selected {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	border-top-left-radius: 6px;
	border-top-right-radius: 6px;
	font-weight: 600;
}

QTabBar::tab:selected:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

QTabBar::tab:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QTabBar::tab:left,
QTabBar::tab:right {
	border-bottom: none;
	padding: 16px 8px;
	min-width: 0px;
	min-height: 52px;
}

QTabBar::tab:left {
	border-right: 2px solid transparent;
}

QTabBar::tab:left:selected {
	border-right: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

QTabBar::tab:right {
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
}

QTabBar::tab:right:selected {
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

QTabBar::close-button {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close.png);
	subcontrol-position: right;
	margin: 2px;
}

QTabBar::close-button:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close_hover.png);
}

QTabBar QToolButton {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-left: 1px solid #8A8378;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- toolbox -- */

/* A toolbox tab is a section heading, so it takes the section-heading rank
   verbatim: 13/700, a 2px leading rule reserved transparent, and the same
   recess-plus-accent mark every other selected thing in the sheet uses. */
QToolBox {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QToolBox::tab {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
	padding: 8px 10px;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 700;
}

QToolBox::tab:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QToolBox::tab:selected {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

QToolBox::tab:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------- item views -- */

/* AN ITEM VIEW IS A WELL, the same shape an input is: bg.void inside a 1px
   edge at the 12px container radius.

   THE WELL HAD NO EDGE. Its fill is bg.void and it sits on bg.panel, which is
   1.279:1 -- so the only thing that could have told you where a list ended was
   its border, and that border was stroke.soft at 1.360:1 against the panel.
   Both under the floor, so in practice a list simply bled into the dock. edge
   #8A8378 is 4.018:1 against the panel outside and 5.138:1 against the well
   inside, so the container now has a shape from both directions.

   PADDING went 4px to 6px with the radius. A 12px corner comes closest to the
   frame corner at 12 - 12/sqrt(2) = 3.515px on both axes, so a square row
   corner sitting 4px in was inside the clip by half a pixel; at 6px it clears
   with room. That is what fixes the
   project-row contrast matrix for free -- over a bg.void well the worst
   reading in the whole five-state selection matrix is 4.88:1, against 3.80:1
   in the previous sheet, which is why the extra text.mid token this sheet used
   to carry has been deleted rather than maintained.

   Selection is an accent WASH over the well and a 2px accent leading rule; in
   the texture and asset browsers the cell contents are the data, so those keep
   an outline instead and are overridden further down. */
QAbstractItemView {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	alternate-background-color: #1D1916;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 12px;
	padding: 6px;
	outline: 0;
	show-decoration-selected: 1;
	selection-background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
	selection-color: #F3F0E9;
}

QAbstractItemView:disabled {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
}

/* DENSITY. The row box was 24 + 6 + 6 = 36px, the tallest row box this sheet
   declared -- two pixels above QTreeView::item, which inherits the same
   min-height 24 and overrode the padding to 5px for 34. 4px of vertical
   padding gives 24 + 4 + 4 = 32, which is four pixels back on every row of
   every list, tree and table in the application.

   THE COMPARISONS PUBLISHED HERE WERE "the tallest interactive row in the
   sheet by three pixels and nine taller than a menu row". The first was wrong
   by one -- 36 - 34 = 2. The second is not derivable from this file at all:
   QMenu::item declares 6px of vertical padding and no min-height, so its box
   is the runtime line box plus 12 and no arithmetic in a style sheet can say
   what that comes to. It is dropped rather than adjusted.

   SAFE FOR THE ROWS THAT CARRY A WIDGET. setItemWidget installs the row as a
   persistent editor whose geometry comes from QStyledItemDelegate::
   updateEditorGeometry via SE_ItemViewItemText, and QStyleSheetStyle SUBTRACTS
   this padding from it -- which is exactly why such rows clip their text. This
   change moves padding DOWN, so it hands those rows four pixels back rather
   than taking any. */
QAbstractItemView::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	padding: 4px 8px;
	min-height: 24px;
}

/* A row hover LIFTS from the well to bg.panel, a 1.279:1 step. */
QAbstractItemView::item:hover {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* Four distinct selection states, not one. Without :!active a panel that has
   lost focus still screams "this row is selected" as loudly as the one you are
   actually working in.

   Measured over the bg.void well: 0.24 alpha composites to #423213, a 1.558:1
   plate carrying text.hi at 10.87:1 and text.dim at 6.23:1. Hovered as well,
   0.34 gives #564117, 1.986:1, text.hi 8.53:1 and text.dim 4.88:1. */
QAbstractItemView::item:selected:active {
	background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QAbstractItemView::item:selected:!active {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	border-left: 2px solid #746D64;
	color: #E4DFD7;
}

QAbstractItemView::item:selected:hover {
	background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.34);
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QAbstractItemView::item:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QListView {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
}

QListView::item {
	min-height: 24px;
}

QTreeView {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	paint-alternating-row-colors-for-empty-area: 0;
}

/* 24 + 4 + 4 = 32, the same box the flat item rule lands on. This rule ties
   with QAbstractItemView::item on specificity and wins on document order, so
   if it is left behind the tree rows sit two pixels taller than the list rows.
   They tie at 1 + 0x10 = 17 each: one element name and one pseudo-ELEMENT. An
   earlier note here said Qt "counts the sub-control for neither", which is the
   wrong reason for the right answer -- Qt appends a pseudo-element to the same
   basicSelectors pseudos list a pseudo-class goes into and scores it +0x10
   like any other (qcssparser.cpp, parseSimpleSelector). It counts ::item for
   BOTH, which is why they tie. */
QTreeView::item {
	padding: 4px 4px;
}

/* No dotted connector lines. The chevron alone is the affordance, which is
   what survives at small sizes. */
QTreeView::branch {
	background: transparent;
	border-image: none;
	image: none;
}

QTreeView::branch:has-children:!has-siblings:closed,
QTreeView::branch:closed:has-children:has-siblings {
	border-image: none;
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_branch_closed.png);
}

QTreeView::branch:open:has-children:!has-siblings,
QTreeView::branch:open:has-children:has-siblings {
	border-image: none;
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_branch_open.png);
}

QTreeView::branch:selected {
	background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
}

QTableView {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	alternate-background-color: #1D1916;
	gridline-color: #403B35;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	selection-background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
	selection-color: #F3F0E9;
}

QTableView QTableCornerButton::section {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	border: none;
	border-right: 1px solid #403B35;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QHeaderView {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

/* The table-header rank: 11/700 text.dim, 5.92:1 on bg.raised. A header is a
   label for a column, not content, so it is the one place in the sheet where a
   700 weight sits at the smallest size.

   IT WAS 39px TALL, the tallest thing in the sheet, carrying 11px text whose
   line box measures 13px: 26 content + 6 + 6 padding + 1 bottom border. That
   is a title bar wearing a header's clothes. min-height 26 -> 16 gives
   1 (the new lit top) + 6 + 16 + 6 + 1 = 30, nine pixels back on every table
   header in the application, and the 13px line box still sits in a 16px
   content rect -- 1.5px of slack inside the content rect, 7.5px from the line
   box to the border once the 6px padding is counted. (It said "four pixels of
   air above and below", which is neither of those two readings and was simply
   not computed. 16 - 13 = 3, halved is 1.5.)

   The lit top edge is the same one-pixel bevel the button and the docked
   toolbar carry, and for the same measured reason: edge.lit is 4.648:1 against
   this bg.raised fill and 3.701:1 against the bg.overlay it hovers to, where
   the stroke.soft seam beneath is 1.06:1. The bottom and right stay
   stroke.soft -- those are grid seams inside a table, not the edge of an
   object, and they do not carry 3:1. */
QHeaderView::section {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #A9A296;
	border-right: 1px solid #403B35;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
	padding: 6px 8px;
	min-height: 16px;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 700;
}

QHeaderView::section:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QHeaderView::section:checked {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

QHeaderView::section:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QHeaderView::section:last,
QHeaderView::section:only-one {
	border-right: none;
}

QHeaderView::down-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
	subcontrol-origin: content;
	subcontrol-position: center right;
	right: 4px;
}

QHeaderView::up-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
	subcontrol-origin: content;
	subcontrol-position: center right;
	right: 4px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------ scroll bars -- */

/* 12px, not 10: ten pixels is under every platform minimum comfortable drag
   target. No arrows and no page fill -- that half was already right and is
   untouched; all four arrow sub-controls stay zeroed below.

   THE TROUGH IS NOW TRANSPARENT AND THAT IS THE VISIBLE HALF OF THIS CHANGE.
   It was an OPAQUE bg.void stripe. Inside an item view, whose well is also
   bg.void, that was invisible and correct. Down the side of a QScrollArea on
   bg.panel -- which is the right-hand tool dock, the surface this application
   is looked at through -- it painted a hard black stripe 12px wide the full
   height of the dock. A transparent trough is the same thing in the well and
   nothing at all on the panel.

   THE THUMB WAS UNDER THE FLOOR ON EVERY SURFACE IT CAN APPEAR ON: stroke
   #565049 measures 2.422:1 on the bg.void well and 1.894:1 on bg.panel. edge
   #8A8378 measures 5.138:1 on void, 4.654:1 on alt, 4.018:1 on panel and
   3.137:1 on raised -- it clears 3:1 on all four, where the token it replaces
   cleared none.

   IT GROWS ON HOVER, which is the overlay-scrollbar idiom and the only kind of
   feedback a bar with no arrows can give: margin 3px -> 2px takes the visible
   thumb from 6px to 8px inside the same 12px track, so nothing reflows and
   nothing else on screen moves. The radius is half the visible width in both
   states -- 3 of 6, 4 of 8 -- so it stays a pill rather than becoming a
   rounded rectangle at one size and a lozenge at the other. Pressed keeps the
   grown size and takes accent, 8.772:1 against the void well. */
QScrollBar:vertical {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	width: 12px;
	margin: 0px;
}

QScrollBar:horizontal {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	height: 12px;
	margin: 0px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:vertical {
	background-color: #8A8378;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
	margin: 3px;
	min-height: 32px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:horizontal {
	background-color: #8A8378;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
	margin: 3px;
	min-width: 32px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:hover {
	background-color: #BFB7AA;
	border-radius: 4px;
	margin: 2px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:pressed {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	border-radius: 4px;
	margin: 2px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:disabled {
	background-color: #292621;
}

QScrollBar::add-line,
QScrollBar::sub-line {
	background: none;
	border: none;
	width: 0px;
	height: 0px;
}

QScrollBar::add-page,
QScrollBar::sub-page {
	background: transparent;
}

QScrollBar::up-arrow,
QScrollBar::down-arrow,
QScrollBar::left-arrow,
QScrollBar::right-arrow {
	background: none;
	image: none;
	width: 0px;
	height: 0px;
}

/* Transparent with the troughs, or the square where two transparent bars meet
   is the one bg.void chip left on a bg.panel scroll area. */
QAbstractScrollArea::corner {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}


/* --------------------------------------------------------------- sliders -- */

/* Every Radius / Inner Radius / Speed row in the tool panels ends in one of
   these, so after the label it is the most repeated control in the editor.
   COUNTED WITH grep OVER src/noggit, src/external excluded, rather than
   estimated: 19 ExtendedSlider construction SITES -- 16 `new ...ExtendedSlider`
   in C++ plus three promoted widgets in BrushStack.ui -- and 26 `new QSlider`
   sites plus five plain QSlider widgets in .ui forms, on top of the one
   QSlider that ExtendedSliderUi.ui hands every ExtendedSlider instance.
   RUNTIME INSTANCES ARE A DIFFERENT NUMBER AND ARE NOT COUNTABLE FROM SOURCE:
   script_settings.cpp:135 is a `new QSlider` inside the ADD_SLIDER macro,
   expanded at :196 and :206 once per script-declared setting, so the scripting
   panel alone contributes an open-ended count. This said "eighteen
   ExtendedSlider rows plus twenty-nine plain QSliders", which matches no
   reading of the tree; sites are what a sheet can honestly count, so sites are
   what it now claims.

   GEOMETRY, AND THE ONE RULE THAT GOVERNS ALL OF IT. QSS width/height size the
   CONTENT rect and the border adds. QStyleSheetStyle places the handle inside
   the groove's contents rect expanded by the handle's negative cross-axis
   margins, and the WIDGET BAND then clips it:

       handle box = declared size + 2 x border width
       handle box = groove box + 2 x |cross-axis margin|
       grabbable  = min(handle box, band), centred

   Groove 6px, handle box 20px, margin -7px: 6 + 7 + 7 = 20. The band is forced
   to 20 by the min-height / min-width below, so the grabbable area is
   20 x 20 = 400px against the 16 x 16 = 256px it was, a 56 percent larger drag
   target on the control that sets brush radius.

   THE COMMENT THAT USED TO SIT HERE WAS WRONG AND IS DELETED, not softened. It
   claimed Qt derives SC_SliderHandle from the WIDGET height and never from the
   QSS handle box, and therefore that this rule changes what is painted but not
   what is grabbable. The repo's own probe disproves it: the sweep recorded in
   the comment block of ExtendedSliderUi.ui -- the eight-row table at lines
   62-70, headed "sheet / band / handle rect / pixels that GRAB the handle" --
   calls QStyle::hitTestComplexControl, the exact call QSlider::mousePressEvent
   uses to choose between a drag and a page-step, against a slider with this
   sheet loaded, and reports a 16x16 handle rect at every band from 16 to 22
   and a rect at y = -1 clipped to 224 grabbable pixels at band 14. The box
   comes from the SHEET and the band only clips it. Both statements cannot be
   true and the probe is the one with data behind it. (The reference here read
   "ExtendedSliderUi.ui:50-56"; the table moved when that file's comment grew
   and it is at 62-70 now. Quoting the table's heading as well as its line
   range so the next drift is findable by search rather than by line count.)

   THE BOX IS 20px IN EVERY STATE AND THE RADIUS IS ALWAYS 10 -- half of 20, so
   a full circle. Every state rule restates size AND border so the two always
   sum to 20; nothing here can resize the grip, drag the row with it, or shift
   the drag offset. Growing the box on hover was measured and rejected twice
   over: a 22px box needs a 22px band at every one of those ExtendedSlider
   sites and in ExtendedSliderUi.ui with it,
   and QSlider computes clickOffset from the PRE-press handle rect while
   pixelPosToRangeValue recomputes sliderLength from the POST-press one, so any
   along-axis size change between the two states jumps the value on grab. The
   lift is carried by RING WEIGHT instead: 2px at rest, 3px focused, 4px
   hovered, 6px pressed.

   COLOUR. The ring is always ink, because it is the only token that clears 3:1
   against both an ivory core and a gold one -- ink/text.hi 16.933:1,
   ink/accent 8.772:1, ink/accent.hi 10.854:1 -- and because it is what
   separates the grip from the accent fill it sits at the end of, 8.772:1
   against sub-page. The old hover put an ACCENT ring on the ivory core:
   #DFA52E against #F3F0E9 is 1.930:1, so it only existed where it happened to
   touch the dark track and the grip itself did not respond at all. Hover now
   moves the CORE to accent.hi, 8.488:1 on bg.panel, and doubles the ring.

   FOCUS IS NEW; there was none, on a control QSlider gives Qt::StrongFocus and
   which is therefore tab-reachable everywhere in the application. It inverts
   the grip -- an ink core inside a 3px accent ring -- so it reads at both ends
   of the track: over the unfilled well the accent ring carries it at 8.772:1,
   over the accent fill the ink core carries it at 8.772:1. Against the
   unfocused appearance the core change alone measures 16.933:1, well over the
   3:1 SC 2.4.13 asks of a focus indicator.

   HONEST LIMIT, stated rather than papered over: the unfilled well is #100E0B
   on a #292621 panel, 1.279:1, under the 3:1 graphical floor. No stroke in
   this palette fixes it -- the best available, stroke.hi, is 2.952:1 on
   bg.panel and edge #8A8378 would read as a light rail contradicting the
   recessed-well depth model this sheet is built on -- so no outline is drawn
   rather than one that would fail anyway. The control stays identifiable
   because the grip is drawn at every value and measures 13.241:1 on bg.panel,
   and the VALUE stays identifiable because sub-page against add-page is
   8.772:1.

   THE ONE DEPENDENCY OUTSIDE THIS FILE IS NOW MET, and the paragraph that
   used to stand here is deleted rather than reworded because it described a
   state that no longer exists. It read: "ExtendedSliderUi.ui pins its QSlider
   to height 16 as a minimum AND a maximum (lines 183-194) ... Until those two
   16s become 20 the eighteen ExtendedSlider grips render as a 20px circle
   clipped to a 16px band." Both 16s ARE 20 in the current tree -- the widget
   is declared at ExtendedSliderUi.ui:195 and the two properties span 196-207,
   with minimumSize height at 199 and maximumSize height at 205 -- so the
   clipped-disc case cannot arise and the line reference was thirteen lines
   stale as well. The two files were changed in the same pass and the note was
   written as if only one of them had been.

   WHAT REMAINS TRUE AND IS THE REASON TO KEEP READING: an explicit maximumSize
   in a .ui is still not something a QSS min-height can beat, so the 20 in that
   file and the 20 here are one number in two places with nothing enforcing it.
   Move either alone and the grip is clipped again. The .ui carries the same
   warning from its side. */
QSlider {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

/* THE BAND. min-height / min-width size the CONTENT rect and QSlider carries
   no border and no padding here, so content == box == 20 and the 20px grip is
   never clipped. This reaches every plain QSlider in the tree with no C++
   change at all. What it CANNOT beat is an explicit maximumSize set in a .ui
   -- see the dependency note above.

   IT DOES, HOWEVER, BEAT A setFixedWidth MADE FROM C++, and the note that used
   to end this paragraph said the opposite: "OpacitySlider's setFixedWidth(35)
   is above 20 on the axis that matters and is unaffected." That was wrong, and
   it broke the texture opacity slider outright. QStyleSheetStyle::setGeometry
   ASSIGNS the minimum rather than raising it (qstylesheetstyle.cpp:2510-2513)
   and it runs at polish, i.e. after the constructor, so min-width 20 replaced
   the 35 the widget had fixed itself to and the hand-painted 35px ramp was
   then clipped seven pixels on one side and eight on the other. The repair is
   an ID rule at QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider further down this file,
   which out-ranks this one 257 to 17 on Qt's own specificity model; the whole
   derivation, including where that model is defined in the Qt source, is in
   the comment on that rule. Any other C++ setFixedWidth/setMinimumWidth on a
   QSlider in this tree is subject to the same overwrite. */
QSlider:horizontal {
	min-height: 20px;
}

QSlider:vertical {
	min-width: 20px;
}

QSlider::groove:horizontal {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
	height: 6px;
	margin: 0px 2px;
}

QSlider::sub-page:horizontal {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
}

QSlider::add-page:horizontal {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
}

/* 16 + 2 + 2 = 20. */
QSlider::handle:horizontal {
	background-color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 2px solid #100E0B;
	border-radius: 10px;
	width: 16px;
	height: 16px;
	margin: -7px 0px;
}

QSlider::groove:vertical {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
	width: 6px;
	margin: 2px 0px;
}

QSlider::sub-page:vertical {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
}

QSlider::add-page:vertical {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
}

QSlider::handle:vertical {
	background-color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 2px solid #100E0B;
	border-radius: 10px;
	height: 16px;
	width: 16px;
	margin: 0px -7px;
}

/* THE STATE RULES ARE ORIENTATION-QUALIFIED ON PURPOSE. QSlider::handle:hover
   and QSlider::handle:vertical carry one pseudo-state each and are therefore
   equal specificity, decided by document order alone -- which is how a base
   rule ends up beating a state rule. Two pseudo-states out-rank both and
   remove the question entirely. The order below is focus, hover, pressed,
   disabled, so a hovered focused grip shows hover and a pressed one shows
   pressed.

   14 + 3 + 3 = 20. */
QSlider::handle:horizontal:focus,
QSlider::handle:vertical:focus {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 3px solid #DFA52E;
	border-radius: 10px;
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
}

/* 12 + 4 + 4 = 20. */
QSlider::handle:horizontal:hover,
QSlider::handle:vertical:hover {
	background-color: #F0BA4A;
	border: 4px solid #100E0B;
	border-radius: 10px;
	width: 12px;
	height: 12px;
}

/* 8 + 6 + 6 = 20. The core shrinks to an 8px dot inside a heavy ring, so the
   grip reads as pushed IN -- and the box never moves, so the drag offset never
   drifts under the finger that is holding it. */
QSlider::handle:horizontal:pressed,
QSlider::handle:vertical:pressed {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	border: 6px solid #100E0B;
	border-radius: 10px;
	width: 8px;
	height: 8px;
}

/* 16 + 2 + 2 = 20. The ring is bg.panel, so on a panel the grip reads as a
   bare 16px grey disc -- quieter than any live state. stroke on bg.panel is
   1.894:1, under the floor, and that IS the disabled signal: WCAG 2.1
   SC 1.4.11 exempts inactive components. */
QSlider::handle:horizontal:disabled,
QSlider::handle:vertical:disabled {
	background-color: #565049;
	border: 2px solid #292621;
	border-radius: 10px;
	width: 16px;
	height: 16px;
}

QSlider::sub-page:horizontal:disabled,
QSlider::add-page:vertical:disabled {
	background-color: #565049;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------- progress bar -- */

/* AN EMPTY BAR USED TO BE NOTHING AT ALL. The track is bg.void and it is
   drawn on bg.panel, 1.279:1, with no border -- so a progress bar at 0% was a
   1.28:1 rectangle nobody could see, and "the operation has not started" and
   "there is no progress bar here" looked identical. The 1px edge is 5.138:1
   against the track and 4.018:1 against the panel, so the bar exists before it
   has anything to report.

   PILL, and the arithmetic is exact. min-height sizes the CONTENT rect, so the
   box is 20 + 1 + 1 = 22 and the radius is 11, half of 22. The chunk sits
   inside the border, so its radius is 11 - 1 = 10, the same inner-minus-border
   derivation the spin-box steppers use.

   THE CHUNK IS NO LONGER A SOLID ACCENT FILL, AND THIS IS AN ACCESSIBILITY
   REPAIR, NOT A RESTYLE. `text-align: center` puts the percentage ON the bar,
   and the previous revision measured the chunk against the TRACK (accent on
   bg.void, 8.772:1) and never against its own TEXT: text #E4DFD7 on accent
   #DFA52E is 1.657:1. The label is centred, so it is fully inside the fill
   from 50 percent plus half the label width onward -- the whole upper half of
   every run -- and the number a progress bar exists to show was unreadable
   there: on every QProgressDialog in the editor, on the minimap render bar
   (MinimapTool.cpp:104), on DownloadFileDialog's progressBar and on the two
   updater bars.

   INK WAS THE OBVIOUS FIX AND IT IS NOT ONE. ink #100E0B on accent is indeed
   8.772:1, but ink IS bg.void: on the unfilled track it is 1.000:1, the same
   colour, invisible. That trades an unreadable second half for an unreadable
   first half, which is worse, because 0 percent is what a load dialog shows
   the moment it appears.

   AND ONE FLAT COLOUR CANNOT SOLVE IT, which is why a background had to move.
   Qt draws this label exactly once. With a ::chunk rule present -- and this
   sheet has one -- QStyleSheetStyle takes the branch

       drawItemText(p, pb->rect, pb->textAlignment | Qt::TextSingleLine,
                    pb->palette, pb->state & State_Enabled, pb->text,
                    QPalette::Text);
       -- qstylesheetstyle.cpp:3996-4000 (case CE_ProgressBarLabel)

   over the whole bar in a single QPalette::Text colour; the two-tone clipped
   pass Fusion does is the OTHER branch and is unreachable without giving up
   the border, the radius and the chunk rule together. So one colour has to
   clear the floor on both surfaces. Solving (L+0.05)/0.054484 =
   0.477955/(L+0.05) for a track at L 0.004484 and a fill at L 0.427955 gives
   L = 0.111372 and a best-possible 2.962:1 in BOTH regions -- the maximum any
   flat label colour can reach over bg.void and accent, and still under 4.5:1.
   The fill is what had to change.

   WHAT IT IS NOW: a bg.hover #5B5651 face carrying the 1px accent rule, which
   is the same "the thing you are acting on gets the accent as a RULE, not as a
   plate" move the tab underline, the item-view leading rule and the active
   tool bar all make. Measured:
     label text #E4DFD7 on the chunk face            5.470:1   (floor 4.5)
     label text #E4DFD7 on the bg.void track        14.532:1
     accent rule against the track                   8.772:1   (floor 3)
     accent rule against the chunk face it edges     3.302:1
   The chunk face alone is 2.657:1 on the track, under the graphical floor,
   which is precisely why the boundary is carried by the 1px accent rule rather
   than by the fill -- the rule clears 3:1 on both sides of itself, so where the
   progress has reached is never in doubt. At 0 percent Qt computes
   chunkWidth = 0 and skips the draw entirely (qstylesheetstyle.cpp:3974), so
   an empty bar shows no stray rule; the 1px edge rule keeps carrying "the bar
   exists" at 5.138:1.

   DISABLED IS EXEMPT AND THE FIGURE IS STILL RECORDED. text.off #7F786A on the
   disabled chunk #565049 is 1.818:1 and on the track 4.402:1. WCAG 2.1
   SC 1.4.3 exempts text that is part of an inactive component, and being under
   the floor IS the disabled signal in this sheet -- the same exemption the
   disabled slider grip is built on. Note the pairing: an earlier reading put
   text.off at 1.993:1 "on the chunk", which is text.off against the ENABLED
   accent; QProgressBar::chunk:disabled carries two pseudo-tokens against the
   base rule's one (33 against 17 on Qt specificity) so a disabled bar never
   shows the accent fill at all. Its rule drops to stroke.soft, the same edge
   every other disabled control in this sheet takes -- 1.739:1 on the track and
   1.393:1 against its own fill, i.e. deliberately absent -- and it is declared
   at 1px rather than removed so the chunk box stays identical enabled and
   disabled and nothing shifts when a bar is greyed out. */
QProgressBar {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 11px;
	text-align: center;
	font-size: 11px;
	min-height: 20px;
}

QProgressBar::chunk {
	background-color: #5B5651;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	border-radius: 10px;
	margin: 0px;
}

QProgressBar:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QProgressBar::chunk:disabled {
	background-color: #565049;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
}

/* THE ONE PROGRESS BAR THAT IS NOT A PROGRESS BAR. ExtendedSliderUi.ui pins
   this one to a 4px maximum height (the widget is declared at line 214 and its
   maximumSize property spans lines 215-220; the height itself is line 218) and
   hides it unless a tablet is driving the value; it is a pressure readout
   drawn as a hairline under the slider, not a job that is running. The rules
   above would give it an 11px radius and a 1px edge on a 4px band, i.e. two
   borders and no interior. Its own radius is 2px, half of 4, which is the same
   pill derivation applied to the size it actually is.

   ITS CHUNK STAYS A SOLID ACCENT FILL, and now has to say so. The generic
   chunk moved to a bg.hover face with a 1px accent rule so that the centred
   percentage can be read on it -- see the derivation above. This bar has NO
   text on it, so it does not carry that cost, and a 1px border on a 4px band
   would be the same two-borders-no-interior failure the paragraph above
   avoids. Solid accent on the bg.void track is 8.772:1 and needs no rule to
   separate it. Specificity is not in doubt: 1 + 0x100 + 0x10 = 273 against the
   generic chunk's 17, on the Qt model named at
   QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider.

   If the object name ever changes this rule goes inert rather than wrong --
   Qt drops a selector that matches nothing -- and the bar falls back to the
   generic rules above. */
QProgressBar#pressureBar {
	border: none;
	border-radius: 2px;
	min-height: 4px;
}

QProgressBar#pressureBar::chunk {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 2px;
}

/* THE ROW LABEL, AND IT IS THE MOST REPEATED LABEL IN THE EDITOR. It is the
   QLabel named "label" in ExtendedSliderUi.ui -- the one setPrefix writes --
   and until the constructor gave it this object name it carried no rank at
   all: it rendered at the global 12/400 text, which is exactly what a check
   box caption sitting next to it renders at. Nineteen construction sites plus
   the open-ended expansion in script_settings.cpp all inherited that.

   text.dim at 11px puts the NAME of the row below its VALUE, which is the
   right order -- the value is the 12/600 text.hi in the spin box at the other
   end of the same row. On the tool-dock card the label measures 7.585:1 and on
   a bg.void dialog ground 9.699:1, both over the 7:1 prose floor.

   NO GEOMETRY HERE. The row height is set by the 20px band and the 20x20 grip
   the block above measures, and an 11px label has a 13px line box, so nothing
   in this rule can change the height of a slider row. */
QLabel#slider-prefix {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 11px;
}


/* -------------------------------------------------------------- tool tip -- */

/* A tool tip floats, so it takes bg.overlay and stroke.hi like every other
   popup. text.hi on it is 8.23:1; a multi-line tip that falls back to body
   text is still 7.07:1. Square corners for the reason spelled out at QMenu.

   NO max-width, DELIBERATELY, and this is the one audit finding refused rather
   than deferred. A long tip does render as one screen-wide line and that is
   genuinely one of the least finished things on screen -- but Qt's tip label
   only turns word wrap ON for a tip it reads as rich text, so constraining a
   PLAIN-text tip with max-width caps the widget without giving the text
   anywhere to go, and the tip is clipped rather than wrapped. A tip you cannot
   finish reading is worse than a wide one. The fix belongs where the tips are
   set: wrap the text, or set wordWrap on the label. */
QToolTip {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 6px 10px;
	font-size: 12px;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------- scroll area seams -- */

/* QScrollArea, its viewport and its content widget are three separate widgets.
   Left alone they pick up three different backgrounds and you get a visible
   seam down the middle of every tool panel. Item views are QAbstractScrollArea
   and not QScrollArea, so they keep the well surface above. */
QScrollArea {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QScrollArea > QWidget > QWidget {
	background: transparent;
}

/* The right-hand tool dock. The ADS name is kept so the rule is still there if
   the Advanced Docking System is ever switched on; the scroll area the tool
   dock actually builds is named "scrollArea" in ToolPanelScroll.ui.

   HONEST LIMIT, restated: whether Qt honours `padding` as a layout inset on a
   QScrollArea is not something this sheet can prove and it was not verified
   against a running editor. The reliable fix for the missing panel margin is
   contentsMargins(12, 8, 12, 8) in the FORM, which is a different file and is
   not owned by this pass. Do not read this rule as evidence that the padding
   is applied. */
QScrollArea#dockWidgetScrollArea,
QScrollArea#scrollArea {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px 4px;
}

QWidget#scrollAreaWidgetContents {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 0px 4px;
}


/* ================================================================ Noggit == */
/* Application-specific selectors. Class names use Qt convention of "--" for
   "::", so Noggit::Ui::PaletteList is Noggit--Ui--PaletteList. The shipped
   themes spelled these with a single dash in the first position, which matches
   nothing; both spellings are listed here so neither is silently dead.       */

/* --- the frameless window title bar ------------------------------------- */

/* Only ever on screen when systemWindowFrame is off, which is not the default.
   Styled all the same, because a half-dressed alternative is worse than no
   alternative. setupUi names the holder "TitleBar" and FramelessWindow.hpp
   sets WA_StyledBackground on it, so this rule paints at all. bg.void: a title
   bar is the outermost chrome there is. */
QWidget#TitleBar {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
}

/* The window-title rank: 15/600 text.hi, 16.93:1 on bg.void. It used to
   inherit the plain 12/400 body rule, so the name of the window carried the
   same weight as a check box caption. */
QLabel[accessibleName="titlebar_title"] {
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	font-size: 15px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding-left: 2px;
}

QLabel[accessibleName="titlebar_icon"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_icon"] {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	margin-left: 8px;
	margin-right: 6px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_restore"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"] {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 0px;
	min-width: 40px;
	padding: 0px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"]:hover,
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"]:hover,
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_restore"]:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"]:pressed,
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"]:pressed,
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_restore"]:pressed {
	background-color: #4A4640;
}

/* Close is the one control that goes red on hover, because it is the one
   control you cannot undo.

   WHY THE FILL IS NOT bad. This button's glyph is a PNG named by
   qproperty-icon, and Qt resolves qproperty-* once at polish time -- it does
   not restate itself when a pseudo-state changes. So ONE pen has to serve the
   resting bar and every fill, and the resting bar is bg.void: an ink glyph is
   1.00:1 there, i.e. invisible, so the pen has to be light and is text.hi.
   The fill is therefore the half that had to move. It was bad #E86F62, on
   which text.hi measures 2.68:1 -- and the comment that used to sit here
   claimed "ink on bad, 6.31:1", describing a glyph this theme has never
   shipped. generate_icons.py writes that pixel as text.hi and says so.

   Measured on what is actually drawn:
     text.hi #F3F0E9 on close.hover #A75047   4.75:1   (was 2.68 on bad)
     text.hi #F3F0E9 on close.press #974840   5.56:1   (was 3.84)
   and the plate itself is a graphical state mark against the bg.void bar, so
   it carries the 3:1 floor rather than 4.5:
     close.hover vs bg.void  3.56:1     close.press vs bg.void  3.04:1
   with close.press 1.17:1 below close.hover so the press still reads as a
   step. Both fills keep bad's hue; they are darker, not greyer. */
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"]:hover {
	background-color: #A75047;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"]:pressed {
	background-color: #974840;
}

/* Real PNG icons rather than Font Awesome glyphs, so the window controls are
   identifiable with no icon font installed. The maximise control has TWO
   names: FramelessWindow.hpp renames it to titlebar_restore whenever the
   window reports itself maximised and repolishes, which is how the icon swaps
   without a setIcon that the next polish would undo. */
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"] {
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_window_minimize.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 12px 12px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"] {
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_window_maximize.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 11px 11px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_restore"] {
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_restore.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 11px 11px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"] {
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_window_close.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 12px 12px;
}

/* --- icon-font buttons --------------------------------------------------- */

/* THESE TWO RULES ARE NOT ORDINARY WIDGET STYLING. They are the only input to
   the pen colour of every icon in the application. Both icon engines resolve
   it by polishing one parentless probe widget carrying one of these accessible
   names and reading its QPalette back -- see `iconPenColor` in
   FontAwesome.hpp -- and because QSS `color` writes WindowText, Text and
   ButtonText together, a sheet gets exactly TWO slots here and there is no
   third. The engine spends them as:

     this rule            QIcon::On   = CHECKED
     the :disabled rule   QIcon::Off  = UNCHECKED, and also QIcon::Disabled,
                          which the engine separates by painting the same pen
                          at ICON_DISABLED_OPACITY = 0.45

   `color` on a QToolButton rule elsewhere in this file colours that button
   TEXT and cannot reach the glyph. Hover is not a slot at all: Qt reports a
   hovered button as QIcon::Active and the engine derives it as
   QColor::lighter(125) on whichever pen the state resolved to.

   OFF is text.dim #BFB7AA. WHY NOT text OR text.hi -- two reasons, both
   measured. First, lighter(125) on text #E4DFD7 CLIPS TO PURE #FFFFFF (HSL
   lightness 0.869 x 1.25 = 1.000) and text.hi clips too, and this palette
   forbids pure white; #BFB7AA has HSL lightness 0.708, lifts to 0.885 and
   lands on #E6E3DD with room to spare, so it is the only candidate in the text
   ramp that gives a working hover at all. Second, thirty-six icons are on
   screen at once (16 tools plus 20 toggles); at `text` they would all sit at
   7.07:1 and the two bars would be the loudest thing in the application.

   MEASURED, per surface an icon actually rests on:

                       OFF #BFB7AA   HOVER #E6E3DD   ON #DFA52E
     bg.void  #100E0B      9.70          15.05          8.77
     bg.alt   #1D1916      8.79          13.63          7.95
     bg.panel #292621      7.58          11.77          6.86
     bg.raised#3C3732      5.92           9.19          5.36
     bg.overlay #4A4640    4.72           7.32          4.27
     bg.hover #5B5651      3.65           5.66          3.30

   The OFF -> HOVER step between the two pens is 1.551:1, a real and visible
   response. OFF -> CHECKED is only 1.106:1 in luminance but 105 degrees in
   hue, and it is never asked to carry the state alone: the checked face drops
   2.057:1 and a 2px or 3px accent rule appears. The pen is the supporting cue.
   Checked-and-hovered derives to #E8BE68, 9.97:1 on bg.alt.

   DISABLED is the OFF pen at 0.45 alpha: #5F5A53 on bg.void (2.82:1), #6C675F
   on bg.panel (2.69:1), #777168 on bg.raised (2.44:1), #7F7970 on bg.overlay
   (2.17:1). Deliberately sub-threshold -- that IS the disabled signal and WCAG
   1.4.3 exempts inactive components. What matters is that it is no longer
   identical to unchecked; the 0.45 alpha now has a 2.2x difference to
   express. */
QWidget[accessibleName="FontNoggitButtonStyle"],
QWidget[accessibleName="FontAwesomeButtonStyle"] {
	color: #DFA52E;
}

QWidget[accessibleName="FontNoggitButtonStyle"]:disabled,
QWidget[accessibleName="FontAwesomeButtonStyle"]:disabled {
	color: #BFB7AA;
}

/* --- the viewport overlay ------------------------------------------------ */

/* The overlay itself is translucent, but its layout holders are plain QWidgets
   and would otherwise paint opaque slabs of panel colour over the 3D view.

   toolbar-action-holder IS THE SAME DEFECT, SIX MORE TIMES, and it was on the
   render the whole time this rule was fixing the other five. Every
   QWidgetAction in ViewToolbar.cpp wraps its content in a bare `new
   QWidget(NULL)` -- SliderAction, PushButtonAction, CheckBoxAction, IconAction,
   SpacerAction and SubToolBarAction -- and a bare QWidget matches nothing in
   this sheet except the global QWidget rule, which is bg.panel #292621. So the
   contextual second row (the climb bar is icon + two check boxes + a slider +
   Reset) rendered as five opaque bg.panel slabs inside a bg.overlay card, a
   1.608:1 step, floating over arbitrary terrain. These five were named and
   fixed; those six were never named, so they were never reached. The six now
   set the object name in their constructors and join this list at
   (1 + 0x100) = 257 against the global rule's 1. */
QWidget#MapViewOverlay,
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder,
QWidget#upperToolbarHolder,
QWidget#secondaryToolbarHolder,
QWidget#leftSecondaryToolbarHolder,
QWidget#toolbar-action-holder {
	background: transparent;
}

/* FLOATING VIEWPORT CARDS. QSS has no box-shadow; the dark fill, the stroke.hi
   edge and the 8px container radius are what separate a card from the render
   behind it. bg.overlay is 2.057:1 lighter than the application own darkest
   chrome and far darker than snow, so against snowy Dun Morogh or sunlit
   Arathi the card never washes out.

   KNOWN DEAD, LEFT IN PLACE: Noggit--Ui--toolbar matches nothing. Qt resolves
   a QSS type selector against metaObject()->className() and Noggit::Ui::toolbar
   carries no Q_OBJECT macro, so its class name is literally "QToolBar". The
   strip is reached by POSITION instead -- QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar
   -- which on Qt's specificity model scores (1 + 0x100) + 1 = 258 against
   QToolBar's 1 and QToolBar:vertical's 1 + 0x10 = 17, and therefore wins by
   cascade rather than by document order. (Published as "QToolBar (1) and
   QToolBar:vertical (257) at 65538": the 257 was the id weight attached to the
   wrong selector and 65538 was a W3C-shaped number. The model, and its place
   in the Qt source, is at QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider; the ordering
   conclusion is unchanged.) */
Noggit--Ui--toolbar,
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar,
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar,
QWidget#gizmoBar {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-radius: 12px;
	padding: 5px;
	spacing: 2px;
	min-height: 0px;
}

/* A DOCKED TOOL BAR INSIDE A FLOATING CARD. SubToolBarAction nests a plain
   `new QToolBar()` inside its holder, and a plain QToolBar matches the docked
   bar rule further up this file: bg.raised #3C3732, a 1px edge.lit #A9A296 lid
   and min-height 35px. Four sub-mode bars are built that way -- texture,
   flatten, light and climb -- so picking a sub-mode drew full docked-toolbar
   chrome, lit top edge and all, inside a bg.overlay card, in a 45px box the
   card then padded to about 55px.

   The card is the object here; the bar inside it is a layout, so it takes no
   fill, no border and no height floor of its own, and the card's own 5px of
   padding is the only inset. Scoping by class name scores 1 + 1 = 2 against
   the bare QToolBar rule's 1 -- and the four sub-bars are horizontal, so
   QToolBar:vertical (17) cannot reach them. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolBar {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	min-height: 0px;
	padding: 0px;
	spacing: 2px;
}

/* --- the left tool strip ------------------------------------------------- */

/* Sixteen editing tools down the left edge of the viewport in one
   QActionGroup, so exactly one is checked. HOVER LIFTS, ACTIVE RECESSES.

     INACTIVE      transparent, the card shows through. Icon text.dim, 4.72:1.
     HOVER         bg.hover #5B5651, a 1.292:1 LIFT over the card, 1px
                   stroke.hi. Icon derives to #E6E3DD, 5.66:1.
     ACTIVE        bg.void #100E0B, a 2.057:1 RECESS below the card, 3px accent
                   leading rule at 4.27:1 against the card, icon accent at
                   8.77:1 on the recessed face. Three simultaneous cues, and
                   against a hovered neighbour that has gone UP one level the
                   direction alone is unmistakable.
     ACTIVE+HOVER  bg.alt #1D1916, 1.863:1 -- still clearly down, but lifted
                   from the resting active state so the hover is felt. Icon
                   accent 7.95:1.
     PRESSED       accent.press fill, the 3px accent rule held at full strength
                   so a click cannot erase the state it is about to change.
                   Icon ink 5.64:1.
     DISABLED      transparent everywhere, no state mark, icon at 0.45 alpha.

   BUTTON BOX 34x34, and it has to be written here because the base QToolButton
   rule min-width/min-height of 24px overrides any setFixedSize in C++. QSS
   min-/max- size the CONTENT rect, so the box arithmetic is
     width  = 3 (left border) + 3 (pad) + 22 (content) + 5 (pad) + 1 = 34
     height = 1 + 4 + 24 + 4 + 1                                     = 34
   Measured with the standalone probe: actual 34x34. The asymmetric horizontal
   padding pays for the 3px leading rule, so the 20px icon still centres.

   Every state restates border 1px and border-left 3px at those exact widths,
   so switching tools cannot reflow the strip; only colours move. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 6px;
	padding: 4px 5px 4px 3px;
	min-width: 22px;
	max-width: 22px;
	min-height: 24px;
	max-height: 24px;
	icon-size: 20px;
}

QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:hover {
	background-color: #5B5651;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
}

QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:checked {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* Both marks at once. Two pseudo-states, so it outranks the two single-state
   rules above whichever order Qt walks them in. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* :pressed ties with :hover on specificity and wins on document order, and
   :checked:pressed ties with :checked:hover the same way, so all four are
   spelled out rather than left to chance. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-left: 3px solid #B8801F;
}

QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:checked:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* Focus, which this bar had none of: on Qt's specificity model the base
   QToolButton:focus rule carries 1 + 0x10 = 17 and this scope carries
   (1 + 0x100) + 1 + (1 + 0x10) = 275, so without an explicit rule here the
   base rule's transparent reserve wins and tabbing into the strip shows
   nothing. (Published as 257 and 65539. Those were neither Qt's numbers nor
   consistent with the 257 quoted at QPushButton[state="primary"] -- they
   dropped the pseudo-class term the other figure counted. Qt's model is set
   out at QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider and every figure in this file
   now uses it. The ordering conclusion is unchanged: 275 beats 17.)

   TWO rules, not one. The single-pseudo :focus ties with :hover, :checked and
   :pressed and wins on document order from this position, which is what makes
   a keyboard focus visible on an inactive tool. But it also RESTATES the 3px
   leading reserve as transparent, so on its own it would erase the accent rule
   that marks the active tool the moment that tool took focus. :checked:focus
   carries two pseudo-states -- 291 against this scope's 275 -- out-ranks it,
   and holds the rule at accent. The BASE QToolButton family now carries the
   same pair for the same reason; this scope keeps its own copies because the
   scope out-ranks the base outright and would otherwise take the base's
   transparent reserve back. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
}

QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:checked:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* Last of the single-pseudo rules, so a disabled tool shows no state mark. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
}

/* The strip group separators sit on bg.overlay, where `stroke` is 1.18:1 and
   simply not there. stroke.hi is 1.84:1. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar::separator {
	background-color: #746D64;
	margin: 4px 5px;
}

/* --- the view-toggle bar ------------------------------------------------- */

/* The 20 checkable buttons across the top of the viewport: Doodads, WMOs, WMO
   doodads, WMO exterior, Terrain, Water, Lines, Hole lines, Wireframe,
   Contours, Climb, Vertex Color, Baked Shadows, Animations, Fog, Flight
   bounds, Models with box, Hidden models, Show Minimap, Details info. Twenty
   is a count of the live add_tool_icon calls in the second ViewToolbar
   constructor; the twenty-first button on the bar is the history QPushButton,
   which is not checkable. EIGHT are ON at startup -- _draw_models, _draw_wmo,
   _draw_wmo_doodads, _draw_wmo_exterior, _draw_terrain, _draw_water,
   _draw_vertex_color and _draw_model_animations, read off the
   BoolToggleProperty initialisers in MapView.h.

   Same recess model as the tool strip, rotated: the rule goes on the BOTTOM
   edge at 2px and the left reserve drops to 1px. Eight recessed dark wells
   with gold glyphs along a bar reads as a row of latched switches, which is
   exactly what it is -- louder than a bare underline, quieter than eight
   saturated boxes, because the loudness is carried by a DARK plate rather than
   by a saturated one.

   BOX 34x34, content-rect arithmetic as above:
     width  = 1 + 6 + 20 + 6 + 1                      = 34
     height = 1 (top) + 5 + 23 + 3 + 2 (bottom rule)  = 34
   The bottom padding is 3px rather than 5px precisely so the 2px rule is paid
   for and the box does not change height between states. Measured 34x34.

   Scoped by CLASS NAME rather than by QToolBar:horizontal, which Qt parses and
   then throws away. The class is safe to scope on: the two other ViewToolbar
   instances are filled entirely with QWidgetActions and contain no
   QToolButton. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 6px;
	padding: 5px 6px 3px 6px;
	min-width: 20px;
	max-width: 20px;
	min-height: 23px;
	max-height: 23px;
	icon-size: 20px;
}

/* OFF, pointer on it: the plate LIFTS to bg.hover, 1.292:1 over the card, with
   a stroke.hi edge at 1.84:1. Icon derives to #E6E3DD, 5.66:1 on the plate. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:hover {
	background-color: #5B5651;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #746D64;
}

/* ON: the face RECESSES to bg.void, 2.057:1 below the card, and the bottom
   rule turns accent at 4.27:1 against the card. Icon accent, 8.77:1. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:checked {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* ON, pointer on it: lifted off the resting ON face to bg.alt (1.863:1 recess,
   so still clearly down) with the stroke.hi edge and the accent rule held.
   Icon accent 7.95:1. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* :pressed ties with :hover and :checked on specificity and wins on document
   order; :checked:pressed ties with :checked:hover and wins the same way. Both
   are spelled out. The ON rule is held at full accent so a press cannot erase
   the state it is about to change. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-left: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #B8801F;
}

Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:checked:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-left: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* Focus, the same two-rule construction the left strip uses and for the same
   reason: the plain :focus rule restates the 2px bottom reserve as transparent
   and would otherwise erase the ON mark of a latched toggle that took focus,
   so :checked:focus holds the bottom rule at accent. Both restate all three
   border widths at their exact values -- 1px, 1px, 2px -- or the 34x34 box
   changes size the moment you tab across the bar. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	border-left: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
}

Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:checked:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	border-left: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* Last of the single-pseudo rules, so it beats :checked on document order and
   a disabled toggle shows no state mark. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
}

Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar::separator {
	background-color: #746D64;
	margin: 5px 4px;
}

QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 6px;
	min-width: 28px;
	min-height: 28px;
	padding: 2px;
	font-weight: 600;
	font-size: 13px;
}

QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton:hover {
	background-color: #5B5651;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* Same recess model as the two bars beside it, so the three read as one
   family: the checked face drops to bg.void and the glyph turns accent. */
QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton:checked {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #DFA52E;
}

/* On Qt's specificity model the base QPushButton:focus rule is 1 + 0x10 = 17
   and this scope is (1 + 0x100) + (1 + 0x10) = 274, so without this rule the
   base would have won and these eight buttons had no focus mark at all.
   accent on the bg.overlay card is 4.265:1. (Published as 257 and 65538,
   which match neither Qt's model nor the 257 quoted elsewhere in this file for
   a single pseudo-class; see QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider for the
   model every figure here now uses. The conclusion is unchanged.)

   NOTE THE TIE THIS SCOPE DOES HAVE: QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton:checked is
   also 274, and this rule is later in document order, so it wins on the
   BORDER. That is harmless only because both declare the identical
   `1px solid #DFA52E`; the checked fill and glyph colour are not restated
   here, so a checked button that takes focus keeps them. Add anything else to
   this rule and it will start eating the checked face. */
QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
}

QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
}

QPushButton#cameraXButton,
QPushButton#cameraYButton,
QPushButton#cameraZButton,
QPushButton#gizmoModeButton,
QPushButton#gizmoRotateButton,
QPushButton#gizmoScaleButton,
QPushButton#gizmoTranslateButton,
QPushButton#gizmoVisibleButton {
	font-weight: 600;
	font-size: 13px;
	border-radius: 6px;
}

/* Overlay sliders and labels are drawn straight over the render. */
QSlider[accessibleName="overlay_slider_vertical"] {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 12px 5px;
}

QSlider[accessibleName="overlay_slider_horizontal"] {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 5px 12px;
}

/* ShaderTool's hue / saturation / value sliders. color_widgets paints its own
   sunken frame and gradient (gradient_slider.cpp:155-177), so no ::groove rule
   written here would ever be drawn -- but line 192 hands the HANDLE back to
   the style, which means the QSlider::handle rules above DO dress it. All this
   rule owes them is room: without a 20px band the new 20px grip is clipped to
   whatever height the layout happens to give them. src/external is vendored
   and must not be edited, so this is the reach-around, and it works because
   both classes carry Q_OBJECT and a Qt type selector therefore resolves them.

   RECORDED, NOT FIXABLE FROM HERE: gradient_slider.cpp:182 clears
   State_HasFocus before handing the option to the style, so the new :focus
   handle rule can never fire on these three. */
color_widgets--GradientSlider,
color_widgets--HueSlider {
	min-height: 20px;
}

QLabel[accessibleName="overlay_label"] {
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	padding: 0px 10px;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: 600;
}

/* --- the tool dock ------------------------------------------------------- */

/* THE GROUND DROPS AND THE SECTIONS BECOME CARDS.

   The right-hand dock was the last region still on the SECOND revision of this
   design language: a bg.panel ground with each section drawn as a 13/700
   text.hi caption over a full-width edge hairline, a dozen of them stacked
   down the panel. Both brand windows had already left that construction
   behind -- the project selector puts bg.panel cards on a bg.void root
   (QWidget#project-card) and the map window does the same on its pages
   (QWidget#map-card) -- and the map window's own source records why it did:
   "THIS REPLACES A QGroupBox ... three nested outlines around one form".

   So the dock takes the same construction, checked against the same numbers
   those two cards were checked against:

     ground   bg.void   #100E0B
     card     bg.panel  #292621   1.279:1 above the ground
     edge     edge      #8A8378   4.018:1 on the card, 5.138:1 on the ground

   The card is an object by BOTH cues, a fill step and a border, which is the
   whole reason the edge ramp exists. 12px radius is the CONTAINER tier -- the
   same radius the two brand cards and the three floating viewport cards take.

   NOTHING INSIDE A CARD CHANGES MEASUREMENT, and that is what makes almost all
   of this a sheet edit. A button is bg.raised #3C3732, 1.281:1 on the card; an
   input well is bg.void #100E0B, 1.279:1 on the card; a slider groove is
   bg.void. Every one of those was already measured against bg.panel, because
   bg.panel is what the dock's ground used to be. Only the ground moved.

   SCOPING, and why it wins by cascade rather than by document order. Qt scores
   a basic selector as 1 for a type name, 0x10 per pseudo-state or attribute
   and 0x100 per id, and sums the basic selectors of a descendant chain
   (qcssparser.cpp, BasicSelector::specificity). ToolPanel carries Q_OBJECT and
   lives in Noggit::Ui::Tools, so the type name resolves.

     Noggit--Ui--Tools--ToolPanel QWidget#scrollHolder   1 + (1 + 0x100) = 258
     QWidget#scrollAreaWidgetContents                        (1 + 0x100) = 257
     QWidget                                                             =   1
     Noggit--Ui--Tools--ToolPanel QGroupBox                      1 + 1   =   2
     QGroupBox                                                           =   1

   The one tie is with "QGroupBox QGroupBox" further up this file, also 2, and
   it is broken deliberately: the nested rule below scores 3 and therefore wins
   on specificity, not on being written later. */

Noggit--Ui--Tools--ToolPanel QWidget#scrollHolder,
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ToolPanel QScrollArea#scrollArea,
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ToolPanel QWidget#scrollAreaWidgetContents {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}

/* BELT AND BRACES FOR THE TOOL WIDGETS THEMSELVES. Each of the sixteen widgets
   ToolPanel::registerTool takes is a direct child of scrollAreaWidgetContents,
   and the global QWidget rule at the top of this file paints bg.panel. Whether
   it reaches a given tool depends on something this sheet cannot see: a plain
   QWidget instance takes a style-sheet background, a QWidget SUBCLASS does not
   unless it sets Qt::WA_StyledBackground or paints one itself -- the same
   mechanism QWidget#TitleBar's note records. Most tool widgets are subclasses
   and would show the new ground through; the impassibility tool's placeholder
   is a bare QWidget and would have painted a bg.panel slab over it. This rule
   settles both cases at 1 + (1 + 0x100) + 1 = 259 without needing to know
   which is which. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ToolPanel QWidget#scrollAreaWidgetContents > QWidget {
	background: transparent;
}

/* THE CARD.

   REFLOW, and it is the one part of this that is NOT free. The base QGroupBox
   rule pads 12px top and bottom and deliberately NOTHING left or right, so
   that "a section costs the panel no width at all". A card cannot do that --
   content flush against its own border is not a card -- so this rule pads 12px
   on all four sides, and the 26px that costs (12 + 12 of padding, 1 + 1 of
   border) is PAID FOR BY THE DOCK rather than taken out of the tool. That is
   the same move ToolPanel.cpp already makes for the panel's own 24px of side
   margin, and for the same reason: TOOL_CONTENT_WIDTH is a contract that every
   tool widget has been measured against (the terrain tool asks 194px, the
   texturing tool's Paint tab 229px) and taking 26px out of a fixed 250px would
   have put a horizontal scroll bar under the most-used tool in the editor at
   the dock's smallest size. ToolPanelScroll.ui's scroll area goes 274 -> 300px
   and ToolPanel's own minimum 289 -> 315px in the same change. The content
   floor is still exactly 250px and every width measured against it stays
   valid.

   The title's margin goes 22 -> 21px because the title's line box goes 17px
   (13px text) to 13px (11px text) and it keeps 3px above and 5px below:
   3 + 13 + 5 = 21, which is exactly the margin the title has to fit inside or
   it would overlap the card's top border.

   THE FONT AND COLOUR OF THE BOX ITSELF ARE LEFT ALONE ON PURPOSE. A QGroupBox
   is a container and QWidget font and colour propagate to children, so moving
   the box's own 13/700 here would re-rank every child that does not carry a
   font rule of its own. Only ::title is re-ranked, and a sub-control rule
   reaches nothing but the sub-control. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ToolPanel QGroupBox {
	background-color: #292621;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 12px;
	margin-top: 21px;
	padding: 12px;
}

/* THE SECTION HEADING TAKES THE LAUNCHER'S RANK, which is the mark that says
   "same product". QLabel#launcher-heading is 11/700 text.dim with a 3px
   brand.crimson tick; this is that rule with the padding a group-box title
   needs. The editor's heading used to be 13/700 text.hi over a 4.018:1
   full-width rule -- two sizes bigger, one rank brighter and louder than the
   brand windows' while saying less.

   text.dim rather than text.hi, on the same argument the launcher makes: a
   heading that names a section is a label for what is under it, and the
   controls are the objects. On the bg.void ground the title sits on it
   measures 9.699:1, over the 7:1 prose floor.

   THE TICK IS LEGITIMATE CRIMSON AND IS NOT A STATE. brand.crimson is barred
   from meaning "selected", "active" or "checked" anywhere in the editor,
   because it sits 1.318:1 and 16.0 degrees from bad #E86F62 while the gold
   accent sits 1.389:1 and 34.5 degrees away, and that separation is the whole
   reason the accent moved off red. This tick is a SECTION MARK -- it is
   present in every state of the section, it never changes, and it carries no
   information a user could mistake for "this one is chosen". On the bg.void
   ground it measures 4.790:1, over the 3:1 graphical floor. On a nested
   section, where it falls on the card fill instead, it measures 3.746:1 and
   still clears it.

   NO CAPITALS AND NO TRACKING, and both are refusals with a reason rather than
   omissions.

   Capitals would have to come from the string, because QSS has no
   text-transform. The one place to do that centrally is toolSection() in
   ToolWidgetStyle.hpp, and it reaches 16 of the 59 sections in this dock -- the
   other 43 are raw `new QGroupBox`, counted per file: LightEditor 10,
   ObjectEditor 10, Water 7, GroundEffectsTool 4, texturing_tool 3,
   AreaTriggerEditor 2, ZoneIDBrowser 2, FlattenTool 2, TerrainTool 1,
   MinimapCreator 1, script_settings 1. Uppercasing the 16 ships a panel
   carrying two spellings of one rank, which is worse than one spelling of a
   rank that is merely not capitalised.

   Tracking has to come from a QFont, because QSS has no letter-spacing either,
   and a QFont set on a QGroupBox propagates to every descendant that has not
   set its own letter spacing -- so tracking one title tracks every label, spin
   box and chip inside that section. The launcher can track its headings
   because they are QLabels, which are leaves. Both fixes are the same sweep
   across the eleven tool widgets and belong in one pass. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ToolPanel QGroupBox::title {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border-left: 3px solid #E5405C;
	padding: 3px 8px 5px 10px;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 700;
}

/* A card inside a card is two nested outlines around one form, which is the
   exact failure the map window's source names. A nested section keeps its
   heading and its tick and gives up the box: no fill, no border, no radius and
   no padding, so it costs the card no width at all. 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 beats both
   the card rule above (2) and the unscoped "QGroupBox QGroupBox" (2). */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ToolPanel QGroupBox QGroupBox {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 0px;
	margin-top: 21px;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* --- the segmented chips ------------------------------------------------- */

/* CHECKED AND FOCUSED WERE THE SAME MARK. The QPushButton block near the top
   of this file states the collision and defers it: QPushButton:checked and
   QPushButton:focus both declare `1px solid #DFA52E` and nothing else
   separates them, so a checked-unfocused chip and an unchecked-FOCUSED one
   carried the identical gold ring and differed only by fill -- bg.void against
   bg.raised, 1.638:1 -- and by text rank, text.hi against text, 1.165:1.
   Neither is a difference. With nine chips in one exclusive group in the
   terrain tool, four in the flatten tool and a third set in the chunk
   manipulator, that collision is on screen permanently.

   The fix is the pattern already proven on the gizmo bar: the checked face
   keeps the recess and the ring and turns its GLYPH accent.

     checked            face bg.void, accent glyph  8.772:1, accent ring on the
                        same face 8.772:1
     focused, unchecked face bg.raised, text glyph  8.873:1, accent ring 5.356:1
     the two faces                                  1.638:1 apart
     the two glyphs                                 1.657:1 apart in luminance

   1.657:1 is not what separates them and this note does not pretend it is:
   what separates them is CHROMA. accent #DFA52E is 79.4% saturated and text
   #E4DFD7 is 5.7% saturated -- one is a colour and the other is white. (Hue is
   the wrong measure here and was quoted wrongly once: the two are 3.4 degrees
   apart in hue, because the hue of a near-neutral is meaningless.)

   Scoped to the segmented sections by the object name ToolWidgetStyle.hpp
   already sets on them, so it cannot reach an ordinary push button anywhere
   else in the application: (1 + 0x100) + (1 + 0x10) = 274 against the base
   :checked and :focus rules at 17 each. */
QGroupBox#toolSegments QPushButton:checked {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #DFA52E;
}

/* A checked chip still answers the pointer, and it has to say so with the same
   one-step lift the base rule uses -- bg.alt #1D1916 -- rather than losing the
   response entirely, which is what would happen if the rule above (274) were
   left to out-rank the base :checked:hover (33) on its own. accent on bg.alt
   is 7.947:1. (1 + 0x100) + (1 + 0x10 + 0x10) = 290. */
QGroupBox#toolSegments QPushButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #DFA52E;
}

/* NINE CHIPS THAT READ AS ONE CONTROL. Every chip inherited the standalone
   QPushButton's 6px radius and sat S1 = 4px from its neighbours, so a control
   the code builds as one exclusive QButtonGroup rendered as nine independent
   rounded buttons. QSS has no :first-child, so a true segmented control needs
   the C++ side to tag edge positions; a 3px radius -- the CONTROL tier's
   smallest step, half the standalone radius -- with the grid gutter dropped
   from 4px to 1px in segmentGrid() gets most of it for two lines. 1px is
   deliberately off the 4px spacing scale: it is a seam inside one control, not
   a gap between siblings, and the scale describes gaps between siblings.

   IT ALSO BUYS WIDTH. The chip arithmetic in ToolWidgetStyle.hpp sums the
   grid's column maxima plus two gutters; 4px -> 1px returns 6px of the 199px
   that row was measured at. */
QGroupBox#toolSegments QPushButton {
	border-radius: 3px;
}


/* --- texture and asset browsers ----------------------------------------- */

/* Selection here must not fill the cell: the cell IS the texture. An accent
   outline reads as selected without hiding what was selected.

   HALF OF THIS IS DEAD, and it is the same Q_OBJECT problem as the left tool
   strip. Noggit::Ui::TextureList has the macro and is reachable;
   Noggit::Ui::PaletteList does not -- only texture_palette_small in that file
   does -- so Qt sees the class as a plain QListWidget and every PaletteList
   rule below is discarded. One Q_OBJECT line in that header makes all four
   live. Reproduce with:
     python check_qss.py theme.qss . --moc <build>/noggit_autogen
   The Noggit-Ui-- single-dash spellings are what the shipped Dark theme used;
   they match nothing in any build and are kept only so a reader comparing the
   two sheets can see the pair. */
Noggit--Ui--PaletteList::item,
Noggit-Ui--PaletteList::item,
Noggit--Ui--TextureList::item,
Noggit-Ui--TextureList::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: 2px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 6px;
	padding: 1px;
}

Noggit--Ui--PaletteList::item:hover,
Noggit-Ui--PaletteList::item:hover,
Noggit--Ui--TextureList::item:hover,
Noggit-Ui--TextureList::item:hover {
	background: transparent;
	border: 2px solid #746D64;
}

Noggit--Ui--PaletteList::item:selected,
Noggit-Ui--PaletteList::item:selected,
Noggit--Ui--TextureList::item:selected,
Noggit-Ui--TextureList::item:selected {
	background: transparent;
	border: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QTreeView[accessibleName="ab_treeview"]::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	padding: 5px 4px;
}

QTreeView[accessibleName="ab_treeview"]::item:hover {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QTreeView[accessibleName="ab_treeview"]::item:selected {
	background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* THE BAND, RESTATED HERE BECAUSE THE WIDGET BAND ABOVE BREAKS THIS CONTROL.
   QSlider:vertical declares min-width 20px, and a QSS minimum is not a floor
   that C++ can raise -- QStyleSheetStyle::setGeometry ASSIGNS it:

       if (geo->minWidth != -1) {
           w->setProperty("_q_stylesheet_minw", true);
           w->setMinimumWidth(rule.boxSize(QSize(qMax(geo->width,
                                                      geo->minWidth), 0)).width());
       }
       -- qstylesheetstyle.cpp:2510-2513, Qt 5.9.9 source tree on this machine

   Polish runs AFTER the constructor, so setFixedWidth(35) in OpacitySlider's
   ctor (texturing_tool.cpp:1028) was being overwritten to a MINIMUM of 20 with
   the maximum left at 35: the widget stopped being fixed. min-width alone does
   not satisfy QRenderRule::hasContentsSize (that needs width/height,
   qstylesheetstyle.cpp:579), so the cross-axis size hint fell through to the
   base style's PM_SliderThickness -- 15 under Fusion (qfusionstyle.cpp:3130),
   16 on QCommonStyle (qcommonstyle.cpp:4505) -- and the layout, which adds the
   slider with Qt::AlignHCenter (texturing_tool.cpp:178), allocated exactly the
   assigned 20px. The paintEvent kept drawing at 35: grooveRect.setLeft(
   (width() - 35) / 2) = -7, so seven pixels of the gradient and the left
   rounded cap fell off one side and eight and the right cap off the other. The
   one control in the app whose groove is DATA rendered as a clipped stub.

   The note that used to sit at QSlider:vertical claimed "OpacitySlider's
   setFixedWidth(35) is above 20 on the axis that matters and is unaffected".
   That was the wrong model of what min-width does; it is deleted there rather
   than softened, and this rule is the repair.

   WHY THIS RULE WINS, WITH THE MODEL NAMED. The authority is Qt's own
   QCss::Selector::specificity() (qcssparser.cpp:1749-1761): per basic selector
   +1 for an element name, +0x10 for EACH pseudo-class and each attribute
   selector -- a pseudo-ELEMENT lands in the same pseudos list, so ::handle
   counts too (parseSimpleSelector, qcssparser.cpp:2487) -- and +0x100 for each
   id. This is NOT the W3C a/b/c model an earlier revision of this file quoted,
   and every specificity figure in this sheet is now Qt's number.

       QSlider:vertical                                        1 +  16  =  17
       QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider                    1 + 256  = 257
       QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]  1 +  16  =  17

   The cascade weight is  order + specificity * 0x100 + (origin + depth) *
   0x100000  (qcssparser.cpp:1983-1985), applied in ascending order, so the ID
   spelling clears the band by (257 - 17) * 0x100 = 61440 against a maximum
   possible order term of 323 -- this file declares 324 rules, so the order
   term runs 0..323. It cannot lose.
   The accessibleName spelling TIES at 17 and wins on document order alone,
   which is sound only because this block sits far BELOW the `-- sliders --`
   section that declares the band. No line number is given for it deliberately:
   two of the stale references this pass had to repair were line numbers into
   files that had grown. Search for `QSlider:vertical`, not for a line.
   texturing_tool.cpp:155 sets the objectName and no accessible name, so the ID
   rule is the one that fires today and the second spelling exists for parity
   with the groove and handle rules below.

   35 IS OPACITY_GROOVE_WIDTH (texturing_tool.cpp:1023). max-width is declared
   as well as min-width so this sheet alone holds the widget at 35 no matter
   when polish runs relative to the constructor; QSlider carries no border and
   no padding here, so content == box == 35 and boxSize() adds nothing. If that
   constant moves, this moves with it. */
QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"] {
	min-width: 35px;
	max-width: 35px;
}

/* The texture brush level slider paints a value ramp; it is the one control in
   the app whose groove is DATA rather than chrome, and therefore the one
   gradient in this file. Neither end is pure: #F2F0EC and #0C0A08, because the
   editor has to stay readable beside a bright viewport. The widget sets this
   name with setObjectName while the shipped sheets only matched accessibleName,
   which is why this control has apparently never rendered as designed. Both
   spellings are here. */
QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::groove:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::groove:vertical {
	background-color: qlineargradient(x1:0.5, y1:0, x2:0.5, y2:1,
	                                  stop:0 #F2F0EC, stop:1 #0C0A08);
	border: 1px solid #565049;
	border-radius: 5px;
	width: 33px;
	margin: 0px;
}

/* THIS CONTROL IS PAINTED BY C++ AND MEASURED BY QSS, which is why it has
   never lined up. OpacitySlider::paintEvent (texturing_tool.cpp:1042) is a
   full override that never routes the groove or the handle through
   drawComplexControl, so nothing here is drawn -- but the same paintEvent asks
   style()->subControlRect(CC_Slider, ...) for its rects, and that IS
   QStyleSheetStyle reading these two rules. The QSS sets the HIT BAND, the C++
   sets the PIXELS, and until now they disagreed: the QSS handle box was
   6 + 1 + 1 = 8px tall against a 6px painted pill, and 34 + 1 + 1 = 36px wide
   against a widget fixed at 35.

   Both are now aligned to the C++ constants the paired change to
   texturing_tool.cpp introduces:
     groove   33 content + 1 + 1 border = 35 = setFixedWidth(35). Was 30, i.e.
              three pixels narrower than the ramp the paintEvent draws.
     handle   29 + 2 + 2 = 33 wide and 8 + 2 + 2 = 12 tall, matching
              HANDLE_WIDTH 33 / HANDLE_HEIGHT 12 / HANDLE_RING 2. The 12px band
              is double the 6px the grip used to offer.
   MARGIN IS 0 AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The handle box (33) equals the groove's
   CONTENTS width (33); if QStyleSheetStyle measures from the groove's BORDER
   box (35) instead, a 33px handle is simply centred with a pixel each side.
   Zero is the only value that cannot overflow under either reading, which
   neither the old -2px nor a positive inset can say.

   The radius stays 5px because it is pinned to Design::RADIUS_CONTROL in
   DesignTokens.hpp, which this pass does not own; if that constant follows the
   sheet's control tier to 6 this line follows it.

   DO NOT TIDY THE GRADIENT OR THE BORDER OUT OF THE GROOVE RULE. QStyleSheet-
   Style only computes QSS rects for CC_Slider when the ::groove rule is
   drawable; strip the background and the geometry stops flowing and every rect
   silently reverts to Fusion's. The handle keeps a background for the same
   reason. */
QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::handle:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::handle:vertical {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	border: 2px solid #100E0B;
	border-radius: 6px;
	height: 8px;
	width: 29px;
	margin: 0px;
}

QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::add-page:vertical,
QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::sub-page:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::add-page:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::sub-page:vertical {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

/* --- main menu / project list ------------------------------------------- */

/* The published column-heading rank, Style::NAME_SECTION_TITLE in UiStyle.hpp.
   17/600 text.hi, one rank above the 15px window title, for a heading that
   names a whole window column rather than a section inside a panel.

   NOTHING WEARS IT AT THE MOMENT. The project-selection window's two headings
   moved to QLabel#launcher-heading at the foot of this file, which is the same
   rank expressed as letterspaced capitals. The rule stays because the name is
   published from UiStyle.hpp as part of the design system's vocabulary, and a
   published handle that resolves to nothing is worse than an unused rule. */
QLabel#project-section-title {
	color: #F3F0E9;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 17px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding: 0px 0px 6px 0px;
}

/* The recent-projects list. THE WELL IS GONE and so are the view's own
   selection states, and both for the same reason: the rows are CARDS now.

   The old shape was a bg.void well with an edge border holding transparent
   rows, so the WELL was the object and a row was a line of text inside it.
   That worked while the window body was bg.panel. The launcher body is bg.void
   now, which makes a bg.void well a box drawn around nothing -- 1.000:1
   against the ground it sits on -- so the well is deleted and each project is
   an object in its own right: a bg.panel fill on the bg.void root, a 1.279:1
   surface step, with an edge #8A8378 border at 4.018:1 against its own fill
   and 5.138:1 against the root.

   SELECTION MOVED TO THE CARD, at QWidget#project-card below. A card is
   opaque, so any wash the view painted underneath it would be invisible --
   which is exactly why the row widget used to be transparent. The state is set
   from RecentProjectsComponent through Style::applyState.

   The 12px bottom margin on ::item is what separates one card from the next;
   ProjectListItem's ITEM_CHROME_HEADROOM is the same number, because the
   delegate takes the margin back off the row it hands the card. */
QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"] {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px;
}

QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"]::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px;
	margin: 0px 0px 12px 0px;
}

QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"]::item:selected:active,
QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"]::item:selected:!active {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

/* The bookmark row. THIS IS NO LONGER THE PROJECT ROW and it is no longer the
   MAP row either: the project-selection window's rows became cards and moved
   to #project-card at the foot of this file, and the map rows became cards in
   the same way and moved to #map-card in the map-selection section below.
   MapBookmarkListItem.cpp is the last file wearing this name, and a bookmark
   IS still a strip inside a list, so everything below is unchanged and still
   correct for it.

   It is a plain QWidget, so it only paints a style sheet background because
   the constructor sets WA_StyledBackground.

   Hover is a TRANSLUCENT lift rather than an opaque plate, deliberately: an
   opaque fill here would cover the selection wash the view painted underneath,
   and hovering the selected row would make it look deselected. */
QWidget#project-list-item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 6px;
}

QWidget#project-list-item:hover {
	background-color: rgba(228, 223, 215, 0.08);
}

QLabel#project-title-label {
	color: #F3F0E9;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 14px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* Back on text.dim: see the five-state table on the list rule above. */
QLabel#project-information {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 11px;
	padding: 0px;
}

QLabel#project-icon-label {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* A favourite or a pin is a marked state, not a warning, so it takes the
   accent: 6.86:1 on the panel and 8.77:1 over the bg.void list well. */
QLabel#project-favorite,
QLabel#project-pinned {
	background: transparent;
	color: #DFA52E;
}

QPushButton#importPathField_browse,
QPushButton#wmvLogPathField_browse {
	margin-left: 8px;
}

/* --- settings window ----------------------------------------------------- */

/* "Changes may not take effect until next launch" is the only thing in the
   window that explains why a setting the user just saved appears to do
   nothing, so it stays at body rank -- 12px text, 11.36:1 on the panel --
   rather than being demoted to a caption. The hierarchy in that footer is
   settled by the Save button taking the primary treatment, which is a
   difference in KIND, not by making the sentence quieter. */
QLabel#_restart_hint {
	color: #E4DFD7;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 12px;
	margin-right: 12px;
}

QPushButton#searchButton {
	padding: 5px 12px;
	margin-right: 4px;
}

/* The collapsible section headers ExpanderWidget builds -- "Custom Brush" and
   friends. It still sets one inline declaration, text-align: left, and that
   wins over this rule for that property alone; it has to, because QPushButton
   has no setAlignment and a theme that said nothing would centre a section
   caption.

   IT TAKES THE SECTION-TITLE RANK AND HAS TO MOVE WHENEVER THAT RANK MOVES, or
   the tool dock ends up with two spellings of "this heads a section" -- which
   is exactly what happened last time, when the group-box heading was re-ranked
   and this one was left behind. That rank is now the launcher's: 11/700
   text.dim with a 3px brand.crimson tick, the same as
   Noggit--Ui--Tools--ToolPanel QGroupBox::title. On the bg.void ground the
   tool column now sits on, text.dim measures 9.699:1 and the tick 4.790:1,
   over the 3:1 graphical floor. The tick is a SECTION MARK and not a state --
   it is present in every state of this button, which is the test the brand
   rank sets for crimson appearing in the editor at all.

   THE FULL-WIDTH border-top IS GONE. It was edge #8A8378, 4.018:1 on the old
   bg.panel ground, and six of these plus a dozen group boxes stacked down one
   dock is the ladder the card construction was adopted to end.

   REFLOW: the header box goes from 1 (top border) + 8 + 17 (13px line box) + 8
   = 34px to 3 + 16 + 5 = 24px, where 16px is the base QPushButton rule's own
   min-height floor on the content rect and is larger than the 13px line box an
   11px font gives. The caret icon the widget swaps in is drawn at the style's
   small-icon metric, which this pass did not measure; if it exceeds 16px the
   content rect follows it and the box grows to match, but it cannot fall below
   24px.

   THE :checked RULE IS GONE BECAUSE IT COULD NEVER HAVE FIRED, and that is a
   source fact rather than a sheet one: expanderwidget.cpp has
   `//m_button->setCheckable(true);` commented out, so the button is not
   checkable and QPushButton:checked cannot match it. The 2px accent leading
   bar it declared has never been on screen. Expanded versus collapsed is
   carried by the caret glyph the widget swaps (FontAwesome caretdown against
   caretright) and by the stack widget appearing beneath. Reinstating the bar
   means one line in expanderwidget.cpp, which this pass does not own. */
QPushButton#__qt__passive_button {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-left: 3px solid #E5405C;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 3px 8px 5px 10px;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 700;
	text-align: left;
}

/* It is still a control, so it still answers the pointer. bg.raised on the
   bg.void ground is a 1.638:1 lift carrying text.hi at 10.339:1. */
QPushButton#__qt__passive_button:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* --- ground effects tool -------------------------------------------------- */

/* The four weight cells under a ground-effect set. The tool used to draw them
   with an inline "1px solid darkGray", the Qt colour name -- #A9A9A9, brighter
   than any text colour in this palette. The list takes NoSelection, so the
   cell is a container and not a control, but a container still has to be
   findable: on the bg.void list well these sit in, stroke measured 2.422:1 and
   edge measures 5.138:1. */
QListWidget[accessibleName="ge_weight_list"]::item {
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 6px;
	padding-right: 6px;
}

/* --- key bindings window ------------------------------------------------- */

/* The seventeen section headings in Help > Key Bindings. The window sets bold
   through QFont and leaves everything else here; it used to carry an inline
   sheet whose margin-left: 150px pushed each heading into the middle of the
   rows it labels. margin-top is the rhythm between one section and the next --
   QSS margins on a QLabel are honoured by the form layout as extra row height,
   which is why the spacing does not need a spacer item. */
QLabel#help-section-header {
	color: #F3F0E9;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 700;
	margin-top: 12px;
	margin-bottom: 4px;
	margin-left: 0px;
}

/* --- map creation wizard ------------------------------------------------- */

QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_add_button"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_remove_button"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_save_button"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_discard_button"] {
	qproperty-iconSize: 14px 14px;
	padding: 5px 12px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_remove_button"] {
	margin-left: 8px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_save_button"] {
	margin-right: 8px;
}

/* --- colour widgets ------------------------------------------------------ */

color_widgets--ColorSelector {
	min-width: 100px;
	max-height: 28px;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 6px;
}

color_widgets--ColorPreview {
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 6px;
}

color_widgets--ColorWheel {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 16px;
}

/* A QLineEdit subclass, so it needs the same padding the base class needs to
   come out 28px tall. */
color_widgets--ColorLineEdit {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 6px;
	padding: 1px 8px;
	min-height: 16px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------- advanced docking system (ADS) -- */

/* Linked but not currently instantiated. Kept short so that if it ever is, it
   does not appear unstyled. */

ads--CDockContainerWidget,
ads--CDockAreaWidget,
ads--CDockWidget {
	background-color: #292621;
	border: none;
}

ads--CDockContainerWidget ads--CDockSplitter::handle {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}

ads--CDockAreaTitleBar {
	background-color: #292621;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
	padding: 6px 12px;
	max-height: 30px;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 700;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab[activeTab="true"] {
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab QLabel {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background: transparent;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab[activeTab="true"] QLabel {
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

ads--CTitleBarButton {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 6px;
	padding: 2px;
}

ads--CTitleBarButton:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
}

QToolButton#tabCloseButton,
QToolButton#dockAreaCloseButton {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 10px 10px;
}

QToolButton#tabsMenuButton,
QToolButton#detachGroupButton {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_undock.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 14px 14px;
}

QToolButton#tabsMenuButton::menu-indicator {
	image: none;
	width: 0px;
	height: 0px;
}

QLabel#floatingTitleLabel,
QLabel#dockWidgetTabLabel,
QLabel#DockWidgetAreaLabel {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 700;
}


/* ============================================================================
   THE PROJECT-SELECTION WINDOW -- THE BRAND RANK
   ============================================================================
   brand.crimson is scoped to THIS WINDOW and to brand furniture, and it has to
   stay that way. Red already means "will destroy your data" everywhere else in
   this application (bad #E86F62), and the accent moved OFF crimson precisely
   because one red could not carry both meanings -- the old accent #E8543F and
   the old error #E0574B measured 1.024:1 apart in luminance and 4.0 degrees in
   hue, i.e. the same colour, which is why red meant eight different things.

   brand.crimson is 16.0 degrees from bad and 50.5 degrees from the accent. The
   16.0 is the same order of separation this sheet already lives with between
   accent and warn (15.8), and it is safe HERE for one checkable reason:

     THERE IS NO DESTRUCTIVE CONTROL ON THIS WINDOW.

   Create, Open, Convert, Settings, and a context menu whose only destructive
   action is commented out. If a [state="danger"] control is ever added to the
   project-selection window, this whole section has to go back to the accent.
   That is the test; it is one grep.

   TWO HARD LIMITS, both measured:

     * text.hi on SOLID brand.crimson is 3.535:1 and fails the 4.5 body floor.
       A solid crimson surface carries INK #100E0B (4.790:1) and nothing else.
       The primary action's LABEL is therefore not on crimson at all -- it sits
       on the tinted fill brand.fill, at 14.940:1.
     * brand.crimson is BARRED FROM bg.raised at 2.925:1, under the 3:1 floor
       for a graphical object. It appears on bg.void (4.790), band.left
       (4.620), band.right (4.939) and bg.panel (3.746) only.

   THE BANNER IS NOT STYLED HERE beyond its colours. BrandBanner paints itself:
   Qt style sheets have no letter-spacing and no text-transform, and no way at
   all to fill glyphs with a gradient, so the wordmark is a QPainter. The
   qproperty- declarations below are how the theme keeps ownership of the
   colours that painter would otherwise hardcode.
   ========================================================================= */

/* The near-black ground the mockup asks for. QMainWindow is already bg.void,
   but the central widget was matching the global QWidget rule and painting
   bg.panel, so the launcher body was panel-grey under a bg.void title bar. */
QWidget#project-selection-root {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}

/* The band, on BOTH brand windows -- this is a type selector, and the compact
   strip at the head of the map-selection window is the same class at its
   Compact scale. It paints an opaque backdrop over its whole rect, so this
   rule is about the colours rather than about the fill; `background:
   transparent` is here so a future revision that leaves a gap does not show
   bg.panel through it. qproperty- resolves ONCE, at polish, which is before
   the first paint -- the widget's setters only have to drop its cached pixmap.

   THE BACKDROP IS ARTWORK NOW and the procedural contour field is gone, which
   is why contourInk has become scrimInk: the colour that used to ink the
   contours now colours the scrim that holds the wordmark legible over the
   picture. banner_backdrop.jpg is cover-fitted to the band at device
   resolution and then held down by two layers whose alphas were SOLVED rather
   than chosen -- a 58% veil in these two band colours over the whole band, and
   an 86% scrimInk scrim under the wordmark.

   THE MEASUREMENTS, because a picture behind text is the one place a palette
   cannot be trusted on its own. The artwork's brightest pixel anywhere is
   #FF9F7A at relative luminance 0.474614, and text.hi on it is 1.759:1 RAW
   with brand.crimson at 2.010:1.

   UNDER THE WORDMARK the veil and the scrim compose to an effective 0.9412 --
   the same figure at every window width, because the scrim is at full strength
   from the left edge to scrim_hold -- and land that worst case on #19120F:
   text.hi 16.260:1, brand.crimson 4.600:1. Over the darkest pixel they land on
   #0C0908: 17.437:1 and 4.932:1. Both floors hold unconditionally.

   PAST THE SCRIM the veil alone leaves text.hi at 7.159:1 over the worst pixel
   a 1180-wide band presents and 6.307:1 at the 800 the launcher can be squeezed
   to -- under the prose floor, over the body floor, and accepted because no
   text is drawn out there. The full arithmetic is at
   LauncherArt::bannerTexture. */
Noggit--Ui--Widget--BrandBanner {
	background: transparent;
	qproperty-bandLeft: #17120F;
	qproperty-bandRight: #0B0908;
	qproperty-scrimInk: #0B0908;
	qproperty-ruleInk: #E5405C;
	qproperty-wordmarkTop: #F3F0E9;
	qproperty-wordmarkMid: #E4DFD7;
	qproperty-wordmarkBottom: #A9A296;
	qproperty-wordmarkAccent: #E5405C;
}

/* --- the section headings ------------------------------------------------ */

/* THE CAPITALS ARE IN THE STRING and the tracking is a QFont, because Qt style
   sheets have neither text-transform nor letter-spacing -- see
   applyHeadingTracking() in NoggitProjectSelectionWindow.cpp for the mechanism
   that makes the tracking survive this sheet's font resolution.

   text.dim rather than text.hi: a heading that names a region is a label for
   what is under it, and the cards and the action buttons are the objects. It
   is 9.699:1 on the bg.void ground, well over the 7:1 prose floor this sheet
   sets for itself.

   The tick is this label's border-left, and it is the one place the mockup
   cannot be followed exactly: a border-left is always the full height of the
   label's box, so it cannot be the short mark the mockup draws. 3px against an
   11px line box with 3px of padding either side gives a 19px tick, which is
   the closest honest approximation. brand.crimson on bg.void is 4.790:1. */
QLabel#launcher-heading {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 700;
	border-left: 3px solid #E5405C;
	padding: 3px 0px 3px 10px;
}

/* stroke, not stroke.soft: 2.422:1 on bg.void against 1.740:1, and this rule
   has to carry across a whole column rather than seam two surfaces inside a
   panel. A seam carries no 3:1 duty -- it is not an object. */
QFrame#launcher-heading-rule {
	background-color: #565049;
	border: none;
	max-height: 1px;
}

/* The row that holds the two of them. A plain QWidget picks up the global
   QWidget background under this sheet, so it has to be told not to. */
QWidget#launcher-heading-row {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

/* --- the action cards ---------------------------------------------------- */

/* A 72px BOX, and the arithmetic matters because QSS min-/max-height size the
   CONTENT rect and not the box:

       1 border + 14 padding + 42 content + 14 padding + 1 border = 72

   BOTH min and max, because the form gives these buttons Preferred vertical
   policy and the column carries an Expanding spacer that would stretch them.
   The 40px icon tile fits the 42px content rect with a pixel either side.

   text-align: left aligns the icon and the label as ONE GROUP --
   QCommonStyle::drawControl(CE_PushButtonLabel) lays them out together with a
   hardcoded 4px between them, which is why the tile pixmap carries 12px of
   transparency on its right rather than this rule trying to space them.

   The fill is bg.panel on the bg.void root, a 1.279:1 surface step, and the
   edge border is 4.018:1 against that fill and 5.138:1 against the root. The
   label is text at 11.364:1. */
QPushButton#launcher-action {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 12px;
	padding: 14px 20px 14px 16px;
	min-height: 42px;
	max-height: 42px;
	font-size: 14px;
	font-weight: 600;
	text-align: left;
}

/* HOVER LIFTS: the fill goes up one surface step to bg.raised (1.281:1) and
   the edge goes to the hover stroke text.dim, 5.922:1 on that fill and 1.888:1
   above the resting edge. Label to text.hi, 10.339:1. */
QPushButton#launcher-action:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #BFB7AA;
}

/* PRESS RECESSES to the floor. text.hi on bg.void is 16.933:1 and the edge is
   5.138:1, so the card is at its most legible at the moment it is clicked. */
QPushButton#launcher-action:pressed {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
}

/* Focus stays GOLD even on this window. Focus means "the thing you are acting
   on" and that is the accent's one job application-wide; making it crimson
   here would be the brand leaking into a system signal. 6.860:1 on the card
   fill. */
QPushButton#launcher-action:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* The disabled card RECEDES to bg.alt and drops to a seam edge. It must not
   share bg.panel with an enabled card, which is what the base QPushButton
   :disabled rule would have given it -- 1.000:1, literally the same surface.
   bg.alt is 1.158:1 below the enabled fill and 1.104:1 below the root, and
   text.off on it is 3.988:1: sub-threshold BY DESIGN. WCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.3
   exempts inactive components, and being under the floor IS the signal. */
QPushButton#launcher-action:disabled {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
}

/* THE PRIMARY. The fill is brand.crimson pre-composited over bg.void at 14%,
   the border is the brand colour at full strength, and the label is text.hi at
   14.940:1 on that fill -- which is the whole point of tinting rather than
   filling: text.hi on SOLID crimson would be 3.535:1 and would fail.

   The border reads 4.226:1 against its own fill and 4.790:1 against the
   bg.void root, so the card's outline clears the 3:1 graphical floor twice
   over.

   THE OUTER GLOW IS NOT HERE. Qt style sheets have no box-shadow -- it is not
   in the Qt 5.15 property list, so it would be silently dropped and
   check_qss.py would report it as an unknown property. It is a
   QGraphicsDropShadowEffect with a zero offset, applied in applyVisualDesign()
   at brand.crimson 40%; the halo's brightest point composites to #65222B,
   1.667:1 above bg.void. */
QPushButton#launcher-action[state="primary"] {
	background-color: #2E1516;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #E5405C;
}

QPushButton#launcher-action[state="primary"]:hover {
	background-color: #3F191D;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #F05A73;
}

/* THE PRESSED BORDER STAYS brand.crimson rather than dropping to a darker
   crimson. A #B82E46 border on this fill measures 2.282:1 -- under the 3:1
   floor, so the card would lose its outline at the moment it is pressed.
   brand.crimson on the same fill is 3.387:1 and clears it. The press is felt
   through the fill instead: 1.248:1 below the resting fill, which is a larger
   step than the hover's 1.108:1. */
QPushButton#launcher-action[state="primary"]:pressed {
	background-color: #501D23;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #E5405C;
}

/* The one place text.hi is a BORDER on this window. On the crimson card a gold
   focus ring would sit 50.5 degrees from a border it has to be told apart
   from; text.hi on the rest fill is 14.940:1 and is unmistakable. This matches
   what the sheet already does for QPushButton[state="primary"]:focus. */
QPushButton#launcher-action[state="primary"]:focus {
	border: 1px solid #F3F0E9;
}

/* --- the gear ------------------------------------------------------------ */

/*     1 border + 8 padding + 22 content + 8 padding + 1 border = 40

   border-left is restated in every state because the base QToolButton rule
   declares `border-left: 3px solid transparent` as a reserve for the active
   marker, and a bare `border:` shorthand in one state but not another reflows
   the button by two pixels as the pointer crosses it. */
QToolButton#launcher-gear {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-left: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 10px;
	padding: 8px;
	min-width: 22px;
	min-height: 22px;
	max-width: 22px;
	max-height: 22px;
}

QToolButton#launcher-gear:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #BFB7AA;
	border-left: 1px solid #BFB7AA;
}

QToolButton#launcher-gear:pressed {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-left: 1px solid #8A8378;
}

QToolButton#launcher-gear:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	border-left: 1px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* --- the project card ---------------------------------------------------- */

/* One project. bg.panel on the bg.void root is a 1.279:1 surface step and the
   edge border is 4.018:1 against the fill and 5.138:1 against the root, so the
   card is an object by both cues rather than by a fill difference alone --
   which is the failure the whole edge ramp exists to fix.

   12px radius: the CONTAINER tier. A card is a container, not a control. */
QWidget#project-card {
	background-color: #292621;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 12px;
}

/* The hover fill is bg.raised, not the half-step it used to be. #34312C against the resting
   #292621 measures 1.164:1, which is under the ~1.28 this palette treats as the smallest step a
   large flat field can carry -- the same threshold that made the previous palette read as one
   surface wearing four names. bg.raised #3C3732 measures 1.281:1 and is a step the eye actually
   reports. The border still does most of the work, but the figure is the border's OWN step from
   #8A8378 to #BFB7AA -- 1.888:1 -- and not the border against the fill it sits on, which is
   5.922:1. A border alone is a thin cue on a card this large. */
QWidget#project-card:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	border: 1px solid #BFB7AA;
}

/* SELECTED, and it has to come after :hover -- the two selectors tie on Qt
   specificity (an element name is +1 and an id and an attribute selector are
   +0x10 each, so both are 1 + 0x10 + 0x10 = 33) and a tie is decided by
   document order. A selected card that loses its state under the pointer would
   be worse than no state at all.

   The state is set from RecentProjectsComponent through Style::applyState,
   because Qt only re-evaluates a rule that selects on a dynamic property after
   an unpolish/polish. */
QWidget#project-card[state="selected"] {
	background-color: #3C2927;
	border: 1px solid #E5405C;
}

QWidget#project-card[state="selected"]:hover {
	background-color: #3C2927;
	border: 1px solid #F05A73;
}

QLabel#project-card-name {
	color: #F3F0E9;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 17px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding: 0px;
}

QLabel#project-card-path {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 11px;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* The expansion is INFORMATION -- an identity, not a location and not an
   action -- so it takes info and not the accent, for the same reason
   QLabel[openExternalLinks="true"] does. 6.289:1 on the card fill, 5.696:1 on
   the selected fill and 5.404:1 on the hover fill. It used to share the path's
   caption rank and colour, which said the two lines were the same kind of
   thing. */
QLabel#project-card-expansion {
	color: #6FAEDC;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding: 0px;
}

QLabel#project-card-date {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 11px;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* A seam inside a container, which is exactly what stroke.soft is for: 1.360:1
   on the card fill. It carries no 3:1 duty because it is not an object -- it
   separates the card's footer from its body and nothing depends on seeing it.
   It is hidden altogether when the project has no readable date. */
QFrame#project-card-separator {
	background-color: #403B35;
	border: none;
	max-height: 1px;
}

/* The three painted marks and the footer row. All of them are pixmaps or plain
   containers, so they only need to be told to stay out of the card's way. */
QLabel#project-card-icon,
QLabel#project-card-art,
QLabel#project-card-date-glyph,
QWidget#project-card-date-row {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* ============================================================================
   THE MAP-SELECTION WINDOW -- THE BRAND RANK, SECOND WINDOW
   ============================================================================
   Everything below is scoped by OBJECT NAME, exactly as the launcher section
   above is, and for the same reason: this is the second and last window that
   wears crimson, and the moment a map is entered the stacked widget swaps this
   page out for MapView. Not one selector here can be reached from a tool dock,
   because not one of them matches without an id this window sets by hand.

   THE SAME TEST APPLIES. brand.crimson is 16.0 degrees from bad #E86F62, and it
   is safe here only while THERE IS NO DESTRUCTIVE CONTROL ON THIS WINDOW. What
   is on it: two tab bars, a name filter, a type and an expansion combo, a
   WMO check box, a list of maps, an add-map button, a settings gear and a
   minimap. The map list's context menu pins and unpins. Nothing deletes. If a
   [state="danger"] control is ever added here, this whole section goes back to
   the accent, and that is one grep.

   THE GROUND IS bg.void, not bg.panel. Every tab page below is turned back to
   #100E0B, which is what lets the map cards reuse the launcher's project-card
   arithmetic verbatim -- a bg.panel fill is a 1.279:1 step on this ground and
   an edge border is 5.138:1 against it. On the sheet's default bg.panel pane
   the cards would have been 1.000:1 against the page they sit on, which is the
   exact failure the launcher's card section exists to describe.
   ========================================================================= */

/* The window ground and the body under the header strip. QMainWindow is
   already bg.void, but a plain QWidget matches the global rule and paints
   bg.panel, so both have to be told. */
QWidget#map-selection-root,
QWidget#map-selection-body {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}

/* --- the header strip ---------------------------------------------------- */

/* THE GEAR SITS ON ARTWORK, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT THE LAUNCHER'S GEAR.
   That one sits on a flat bg.void ground, where the edge ramp's #8A8378 reads
   5.138:1 and is the right resting weight. This one sits on the veiled
   backdrop, and the worst it can be asked to sit on was found by compositing
   the real pipeline over the real asset at every width from 600 to 3840 and
   at device pixel ratios 1, 1.25, 1.5 and 2: the brightest pixel that survives
   the veil anywhere on a 64-logical strip is #703638, relative luminance
   0.063686. #8A8378 against that measures 2.462:1, under the 3:1 floor for a
   border, so the edge ramp cannot be used here.

   The button takes the next step up instead. text.dim is 4.648:1 against that
   worst-case pixel and 7.585:1 against its own bg.panel fill, so the border
   clears the floor on BOTH sides of itself no matter what the picture is doing
   underneath. text.hi on the same worst case is 8.115:1, which is why the hover
   state can go one step further still.

       1 border + 7 padding + 18 content + 7 padding + 1 border = 34
   in a 64px strip, which leaves 15 of air above and below.

   border-left is restated in every state because the base QToolButton rule
   declares `border-left: 3px solid transparent` as a reserve for the active
   marker, and a bare `border:` shorthand in one state but not another reflows
   the button by two pixels as the pointer crosses it. */
QToolButton#map-header-gear {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: 1px solid #BFB7AA;
	border-left: 1px solid #BFB7AA;
	border-radius: 9px;
	padding: 7px;
	min-width: 18px;
	min-height: 18px;
	max-width: 18px;
	max-height: 18px;
}

/* Hover lifts the fill one step to bg.raised and the border to text.hi:
   10.339:1 on that fill and 8.115:1 against the worst the strip can put behind
   it at any width. */
QToolButton#map-header-gear:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #F3F0E9;
	border-left: 1px solid #F3F0E9;
}

QToolButton#map-header-gear:pressed {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #BFB7AA;
	border-left: 1px solid #BFB7AA;
}

/* Focus stays GOLD on this window too. Focus means "the thing you are acting
   on" and that is the accent's one job application-wide. 6.860:1 on the
   button's own bg.panel fill and 4.204:1 against the worst veiled pixel the
   strip can put behind it, so the ring clears the 3:1 floor on both sides of
   itself at every size this window has. */
QToolButton#map-header-gear:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	border-left: 1px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* --- the two tab bars ---------------------------------------------------- */

/* THE SELECTED TAB IS AN UNDERLINE, NOT A FILLED TAB. The base QTabBar rule
   already marks selection with a 2px bottom border -- in the accent, over a
   bg.raised plate with rounded top corners. Here the plate goes and the border
   turns crimson: on this window a tab is a place, and a filled tab that also
   carries the brand colour would be two signals for one fact.

   4.790:1 on the bg.void ground, over the 3:1 floor for a graphical mark.

   The pane keeps ONE hairline so the underline has a rule to sit on.
   stroke.soft at 1.739:1 on bg.void -- a seam, which carries no 3:1 duty
   because it is not an object. */
QTabWidget#map-entry-tabs::pane,
QTabWidget#map-detail-tabs::pane {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #403B35;
	border-radius: 0px;
	top: -1px;
}

QTabWidget#map-entry-tabs QTabBar::tab,
QTabWidget#map-detail-tabs QTabBar::tab {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 10px 14px;
	min-width: 0px;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: 400;
}

/* :hover BEFORE :selected. The two selectors are structurally identical -- the
   same ancestor, the same element, the same sub-control, one pseudo-state each
   -- so whatever Qt computes for one it computes for the other, they tie, and a
   tie is settled by document order. A selected tab that lost its underline the
   moment the pointer crossed it would be worse than no state at all, so the
   selected rule is the one written second. This is the same ordering the
   project-card rules above depend on.

   The hover mark is stroke.hi, 3.775:1 on bg.void: present, and unmistakably
   not the crimson. */
QTabWidget#map-entry-tabs QTabBar::tab:hover,
QTabWidget#map-detail-tabs QTabBar::tab:hover {
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #746D64;
}

QTabWidget#map-entry-tabs QTabBar::tab:selected,
QTabWidget#map-detail-tabs QTabBar::tab:selected {
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #E5405C;
	font-weight: 600;
}

/* Two pseudo-states outrank one, so this needs no ordering argument. 5.887:1
   on bg.void and 1.229:1 above the resting crimson, which is the same step the
   launcher's brand border takes on hover. */
QTabWidget#map-entry-tabs QTabBar::tab:selected:hover,
QTabWidget#map-detail-tabs QTabBar::tab:selected:hover {
	border-bottom: 2px solid #F05A73;
}

/* --- the pages ----------------------------------------------------------- */

/* Back to the bg.void ground. See the note at the head of this section for why
   the pages are not left on the pane's default bg.panel. */
QWidget#map-maps-page,
QWidget#map-bookmarks-page,
QWidget#map-detail-page {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}

/* --- the SEARCH section heading ------------------------------------------ */

/* The capitals are in the string and the tracking is a QFont, because Qt style
   sheets have neither text-transform nor letter-spacing -- see applyTracking()
   in NoggitWindow.cpp for the mechanism that makes the tracking survive this
   sheet's font resolution.

   text.dim rather than text.hi: a heading that names a region is a label for
   what is under it, and the fields below it are the objects. 9.699:1 on the
   bg.void ground, well over the 7:1 prose floor this sheet sets for itself.

   THIS REPLACED A QGroupBox. A group box draws a titled frame around its
   contents, which put a second box inside a column that is already a panel
   inside a tab. */
QLabel#map-section-heading {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 700;
	padding: 0px;
}

QFrame#map-section-rule {
	background-color: #403B35;
	border: none;
	max-height: 1px;
}

QWidget#map-section-row,
QLabel#map-section-glyph {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* --- the map list -------------------------------------------------------- */

/* THE WELL IS GONE, and for the same reason it went on the launcher: the rows
   are CARDS now. A bg.void well on a bg.void page is a box drawn around
   nothing -- 1.000:1 against the ground it sits on.

   The 12px bottom margin on ::item is what separates one card from the next,
   and it is the same number as MapListItem's ITEM_CHROME_HEADROOM, because the
   delegate takes the margin back off the row it hands the card. */
QListWidget#map-list,
QListWidget#bookmark-list {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px;
}

QListWidget#map-list::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px;
	margin: 0px 0px 12px 0px;
}

/* SELECTION MOVED TO THE CARD. A card is opaque, so any wash the view painted
   underneath it would be invisible -- which is exactly why the old row widget
   was transparent. The state is set from NoggitWindow's currentItemChanged
   handler through Style::applyState. */
QListWidget#map-list::item:hover,
QListWidget#map-list::item:selected:active,
QListWidget#map-list::item:selected:!active,
QListWidget#map-list::item:selected:hover {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

/* The bookmark rows are still STRIPS -- MapBookmarkListItem is transparent and
   wears the old #project-list-item rank -- so that list keeps the view's own
   hover and selection states from the base QAbstractItemView rules and only
   loses its frame here.

   4px, and the number is not free: the delegate subtracts the item's padding
   AND its margin from the geometry it hands a persistent editor, the base
   ::item rule already spends 4px of padding top and bottom, and
   MapBookmarkListItem::ITEM_CHROME_HEADROOM is 12. 4 + 4 + 4 = 12 exactly, so
   this is the last pixel of separation those rows can be given before their
   descenders start to clip. */
QListWidget#bookmark-list::item {
	margin: 0px 0px 4px 0px;
}

/* A THIN CRIMSON THUMB. 8px wide with a 2px margin leaves a 4px bar, which is
   a third of the 12px handle the rest of the application uses: this list is a
   column of cards and a full-weight scroll bar beside them reads as a second
   border. brand.crimson is 4.790:1 on the bg.void well. */
QListWidget#map-list QScrollBar:vertical,
QListWidget#bookmark-list QScrollBar:vertical {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	width: 8px;
	margin: 0px;
}

QListWidget#map-list QScrollBar::handle:vertical,
QListWidget#bookmark-list QScrollBar::handle:vertical {
	background-color: #E5405C;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 2px;
	margin: 2px;
	min-height: 32px;
}

QListWidget#map-list QScrollBar::handle:vertical:hover,
QListWidget#bookmark-list QScrollBar::handle:vertical:hover {
	background-color: #F05A73;
}

/* --- one map card -------------------------------------------------------- */

/* bg.panel on the bg.void page is a 1.279:1 surface step and the edge border
   is 4.018:1 against the fill and 5.138:1 against the page, so the card is an
   object by both cues rather than by a fill difference alone.

   12px radius: the CONTAINER tier. A card is a container, not a control. */
QWidget#map-card {
	background-color: #292621;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 12px;
}

QWidget#map-card:hover {
	background-color: #34312C;
	border: 1px solid #BFB7AA;
}

/* SELECTED, and it has to come after :hover -- the two tie on Qt specificity
   (an element name is +1 and an id and an attribute selector are +0x10 each,
   so both are 1 + 0x10 + 0x10 = 33) and a tie is decided by document order.

   card.select #3C2927 is brand.crimson at 10% over bg.panel: text.hi 11.992:1,
   text.dim 6.869:1, and the crimson border on it 3.392:1. */
QWidget#map-card[state="selected"] {
	background-color: #3C2927;
	border: 1px solid #E5405C;
}

QWidget#map-card[state="selected"]:hover {
	background-color: #3C2927;
	border: 1px solid #F05A73;
}

QLabel#map-card-name {
	color: #F3F0E9;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 14px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding: 0px;
}

QLabel#map-card-id {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 11px;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* THE TYPE BADGE IS A CHIP, not a second caption. bg.alt inside the card's
   bg.panel is a 1.158:1 step DOWN -- recessed, which is what a label applied to
   an object should be -- and text.dim on it is 8.787:1. The border is
   stroke.soft at 1.575:1 on the chip: a seam that closes the shape, carrying no
   3:1 duty because the chip is already told apart by its fill.

   11px radius on a 22px box is a pill:
       1 border + 3 padding + 14 line box + 3 padding + 1 border = 22 */
QLabel#map-card-badge {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background-color: #1D1916;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
	border-radius: 11px;
	padding: 3px 10px;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 600;
}

/* The emblem and the chevron are painted pixmaps; they only need to be told to
   stay out of the card's way. Both are drawn at the widget's device pixel
   ratio in MapSelectionArt -- the ring's stroke.hi is 3.775:1 on the bg.void
   disc and the chevron's text.off is 3.443:1 on the card fill. */
QLabel#map-card-emblem,
QLabel#map-card-chevron {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* --- the add-map button -------------------------------------------------- */

/* THE ONE BRAND-FILLED CONTROL ON THIS WINDOW, and the same construction as
   the launcher's primary action: the fill is brand.crimson pre-composited over
   bg.void at 14%, the border is the brand colour at full strength, and the
   label is text.hi at 14.940:1 on that fill. That is the whole point of
   tinting rather than filling -- text.hi on SOLID crimson is 3.535:1 and would
   fail the body floor.

   The border reads 4.226:1 against its own fill and 4.790:1 against the
   bg.void page, so the outline clears the 3:1 graphical floor twice over.

       1 border + 10 padding + 18 content + 10 padding + 1 border = 40

   The label is letterspaced capitals, and both halves are done in C++: the
   capitals live in the translated string and the tracking is a QFont, because
   this sheet has neither text-transform nor letter-spacing. */
QPushButton#map-add-button {
	background-color: #2E1516;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #E5405C;
	border-radius: 10px;
	padding: 10px 16px;
	min-height: 18px;
	max-height: 18px;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: 600;
	text-align: center;
}

QPushButton#map-add-button:hover {
	background-color: #3F191D;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #F05A73;
}

/* THE PRESSED BORDER STAYS brand.crimson rather than dropping to a darker red:
   on this fill brand.crimson is 3.387:1 and clears the floor, and the press is
   felt through the fill instead -- 1.248:1 below the resting fill, a larger
   step than the hover's 1.108:1. */
QPushButton#map-add-button:pressed {
	background-color: #501D23;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #E5405C;
}

/* text.hi as a border, matching the launcher's primary action: a gold focus
   ring here would sit 50.5 degrees from a border it has to be told apart from,
   where text.hi on the rest fill is 14.940:1 and is unmistakable. */
QPushButton#map-add-button:focus {
	border: 1px solid #F3F0E9;
}

/* --- the right pane's preview panel -------------------------------------- */

/* THE PANEL IS THE FRAME NOW, and the minimap holder inside it is not. bg.alt
   on the bg.void page is a 1.104:1 step and the edge border is 4.654:1 against
   that fill and 5.138:1 against the page.

   Both pages of the panel -- the empty state and the minimap -- live in one
   QStackedLayout, which sizes itself to the larger of the two and keeps that
   size whichever is current, so selecting a map cannot make the pane jump. */
QWidget#map-preview-panel {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	border: 1px solid #8A8378;
	border-radius: 12px;
}

/* The scroll area that holds the minimap, and its VIEWPORT, which is a
   separate widget and would otherwise match the global QWidget rule and paint
   a bg.panel rectangle inside this panel. This rule used to give the scroll
   area its own bg.void fill and edge border -- it was the framed object on
   this pane before the panel existed, and two frames around one picture is one
   frame too many. */
QWidget[accessibleName="main_menu_minimap_holder"],
QWidget#map-preview-viewport,
QWidget#map-preview-empty {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 0px;
}

/* The empty state. text.dim on the panel is 8.787:1; the pin beside it is
   painted in text.off at 3.988:1, which is over the 3:1 graphical floor and
   deliberately UNDER the 4.5 body floor -- it is a decoration behind a
   caption, not a second caption. */
QLabel#map-preview-caption {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding: 0px;
}

QLabel#map-preview-pin {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* --- the detail head and the hint row ------------------------------------ */

/* The title's SIZE comes from Style::applyRank as a font-only widget sheet and
   its COLOUR from QLabel[state="value"], which is the sheet's published route
   to text.hi: 16.933:1 on the bg.void page. This rule only stops the label
   painting the global QWidget background behind itself. */
QLabel#map-detail-title,
QWidget#map-detail-hint-row,
QLabel#map-detail-hint-glyph {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px;
}
